WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.730
- Right, good morning everybody.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.050
Good morning to everyone in the room

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.060
and good morning to everyone
at the back of the room

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.150
and good morning to everyone
who's watching this online.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.218
You are all very welcome.

00:00:14.218 --> 00:00:17.730
So this is the board
meeting of NHS England.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.820
My name is Penny Dash and
I'm chair of the board.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.040
And I'll ask my colleagues on the board

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.710
to quickly go round and
introduce themselves.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.573
So Miranda, can we start with you?

00:00:29.346 --> 00:00:30.790
- I'm Miranda Carter,

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.960
I'm Director of System
Architecture in NHS England.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.720
- Hello, I'm Rob Checketts,
I'm strategic advisor

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.900
and responsible for comms and engagement.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.180
- Mark Bailie, non-executive director.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.240
- Claire Fuller, National
medical Director, Primary Care.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.920
- Mary Watkins, non-executive director.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.773
- Duncan Burton, Chief
Nursing Officer for England.

00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:54.474
- Simon Wessely, non-exec.

00:00:54.474 --> 00:00:55.800
- Elizabeth O'Mahony, CFO.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.750
- Jim Mackey, Chief Exec.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.310
- Andrew Morris, Deputy Chair.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.470
- Mark Walport, non-executive director.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.450
- Steve Powis, National Medical Director.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.451
- Jane Ellison, non-executive director.

00:01:05.451 --> 00:01:08.520
- Vin Diwakar, Clinical
Transformation Director.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.190
- Robert Lechler, non-executive director.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.040
- Tom Kibasi, Executive
Director of Strategy.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.780
- Mark Cubbon, Director of Electives,

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.000
Cancer and Diagnostics.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.586
- Meghana Pandit, Co
National Medical Director,

00:01:20.586 --> 00:01:21.419
secondary Care.

00:01:21.419 --> 00:01:23.860
- Great, so we have some
apologies for today.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.860
We've got apologies from Mike
Coupe, Suresh Viswanathan,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.280
Jeremy Townsend, David
Probert, Sarah-Jane Marsh,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.683
Glen Burley, and Amanda Doyle.

00:01:35.585 --> 00:01:38.310
Does anyone have any
declarations of interest

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:39.780
that they haven't already declared

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.580
that they would like to raise today?

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.540
Nope, okay, great, thank you.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.190
I'd like to refer to the minutes

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.510
from the previous board meeting,

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.842
which was held at the end of March

00:01:50.842 --> 00:01:54.540
and see if anyone has
any comments on those

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.255
and be particularly helpful for the people

00:01:57.255 --> 00:01:58.350
who were at that board meeting

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.083
to see if they've got any comments.

00:02:02.938 --> 00:02:05.438
Does anyone have anything
that they would like to raise?

00:02:05.438 --> 00:02:06.925
No, okay, thank you very much.

00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:08.100
So we will approve those.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.130
So I'm going to hand over
to Jim to share with us

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.230
an update on performance

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.470
and he'll do that jointly with Elizabeth.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.058
- Thanks Penny.

00:02:18.058 --> 00:02:20.550
So first of all, just a
couple of other comments

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.860
before we start talking
about integrated performance.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.668
I think today we should acknowledge

00:02:25.668 --> 00:02:29.227
the terrible events in
Liverpool earlier this week

00:02:29.227 --> 00:02:30.630
and the fantastic efforts of NHS

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.360
and other emergency service colleagues

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:34.590
in responding to that.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:37.770
Sadly, the service has become
too used to having to deal

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.440
with these things,

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.913
but they handle it fantastically well

00:02:41.913 --> 00:02:43.470
in pretty awful circumstances.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:44.553
So thanks to them.

00:02:45.807 --> 00:02:47.250
And also this last few weeks, just thanks

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.949
to everybody internally
across the DHSC, government

00:02:50.949 --> 00:02:52.296
right through into the NHS

00:02:52.296 --> 00:02:53.250
for the way everyone's pulled together

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.272
to deal with the challenges

00:02:55.272 --> 00:02:57.660
that we've got in a very
difficult set of circumstances

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.570
as it could be seen in the last board

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.950
where we were in the financial planning

00:03:03.888 --> 00:03:05.629
and operational planning

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:06.870
round with a potentially huge deficit

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.690
and a drift on operational performance.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.480
And I think we've seen a really
strong leadership response

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.600
as you'll see as you go
through the discussion today,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.940
we're talk about planning
and other things,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.330
but we've got an awful lot still to do.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.490
We are trying to govern our work

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.850
as the new team guiding
us through this process

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.680
around a few key themes.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.520
So firstly, trying to work
differently with each other,

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.867
with the service, with colleagues

00:03:34.867 --> 00:03:35.700
across government, et cetera.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.800
Really need to pull it
together, be more agile,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.800
maybe be more collaborative,
be more focused

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.630
on what we need to deliver
in the circumstances

00:03:44.704 --> 00:03:45.600
and prioritising the changes

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.332
that are really going
to impact day to day.

00:03:48.332 --> 00:03:49.710
A lot of what we're
talking about at the minute

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.547
is very long term stuff, understandably,

00:03:51.547 --> 00:03:52.905
and 10 year plan, et cetera.

00:03:52.905 --> 00:03:54.810
But really trying to deliver with urgency,

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.810
things that can make an impact today.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.955
And then also, and I think
this is really important,

00:04:00.955 --> 00:04:02.694
especially when we talk
about the longer term

00:04:02.694 --> 00:04:03.992
and 10 year plan,

00:04:03.992 --> 00:04:06.031
trying to generate ambition
across the service.

00:04:06.031 --> 00:04:09.565
So we've all got ambition for our service,

00:04:09.565 --> 00:04:11.490
for patients, for colleagues to improve

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.700
what we're delivering every day forever

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.360
and really try and raise sights there.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.520
When you look at performance
in the round I think,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.160
will go through the agenda,

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.010
you'll see the improvement in plans

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:29.372
that I've just referenced

00:04:29.372 --> 00:04:30.440
and we'll get to that item,

00:04:32.068 --> 00:04:33.420
really strong elective performance

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.670
over recent months on long waits

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.361
and Mark will come in a second as well.

00:04:39.361 --> 00:04:41.715
We've had a pivot recently as well

00:04:41.715 --> 00:04:43.580
moving away from long waits

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:47.580
and more towards list size
and RTT performance again

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.470
and not in published statistics

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.600
but in more the management

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.976
information we get every week.

00:04:53.976 --> 00:04:54.809
There's some really
encouraging signs there

00:04:54.809 --> 00:04:57.573
which Mark will touch on. On urgent care.

00:04:58.469 --> 00:04:59.604
Still a long way to go

00:04:59.604 --> 00:05:01.560
but I think strong improvement
on ambulance response time,

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.360
some improvement on 12 hour waits

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.780
but not quite where we
want to be on four hours.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.930
And I think some really
strong signs in cancer

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.730
and also in primary care access.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.370
So patient view of primary care access,

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.700
which I think will be some
statistics published also today

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:22.020
that will show really significant
improvement year on year.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.090
So we clearly want to go further

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:28.264
and harder on on all of this
as we'll go through the year.

00:05:28.264 --> 00:05:29.790
But I think a reasonable start

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.793
given quite a tricky position

00:05:32.793 --> 00:05:36.180
from where we were in the planning round.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.705
So I'll probably stop there

00:05:37.705 --> 00:05:38.890
and then Mark if there's anything you want

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:40.170
to add from an electives perspective.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.597
- Thank you Jim.

00:05:41.597 --> 00:05:43.440
Should I just add a few more details

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.660
about the scale of
improvement that we have seen?

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.690
So if I start with the long wait position

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.000
and last year was obviously the final year

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.010
where we were having a significant focus

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.440
on the tail end of the waiting list.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.680
We saw an 85% reduction
by the end of the year

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.230
in our 65 week waits.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:04.350
We also saw a 40% reduction
with our 52 week waits

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.442
with the number of people
that have been waiting

00:06:06.442 --> 00:06:08.910
for the longest amounts of time.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.280
So good progress what
we acknowledge the fact

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.660
that we need to do more

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:14.580
and we're planning to do more this year.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.040
So as Jim you mentioned about this pivot

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.790
to the RTT position, it
is a significant change

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.760
but one that's the first year
of our elective reform plan

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.050
that will get us in four
years to the 92% position

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.106
that need to deliver.

00:06:30.106 --> 00:06:33.930
So if we think at the
moment we're about 60%,

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:37.320
well we're going to ask to get to 65%

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.360
at aggregate by the end of the year

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:40.942
and that will require

00:06:40.942 --> 00:06:43.260
at least a 5% improvement
from organisations.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.300
And for those that are furthest away,

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.110
they'll need to deliver up
to, get to a minimum of 60%.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.640
At the same time we want to keep a really

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.534
close eye on the long waits.

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:53.910
We know how hard it is
to recover the long wait

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.680
if that position moves out.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.540
And we're asking organisations

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.430
and systems to get to
a waiting list position

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:02.340
where we've got no more than
1% of the total waiting list

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.203
for long waiting patients.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.990
So where are we at the minute?

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.120
So as we exit the year,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.182
this was obviously based on the position

00:07:11.182 --> 00:07:15.300
at the end of March we are
about nationally about 60%

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.070
for the RTT position.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.200
So we've got that 5% improvement

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.990
to make on aggregate throughout the year.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.090
We've got the starting point

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.569
for our long waits is about 2.4%

00:07:26.569 --> 00:07:28.496
and we need to get that down to 1%.

00:07:28.496 --> 00:07:29.329
So again, that tight focus

00:07:29.329 --> 00:07:32.340
is going to be really critical for us.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.300
And all of that of course

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.160
is to make sure we're
seeing patients more quickly

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.613
and our longer waiting
patients more quickly as well.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:41.520
So we've also seen, Jim, you mentioned

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.920
about the changes in the
waiting list position,

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:45.870
we see some really good
progress last year.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:47.580
So for six months of the year last year

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.760
we saw an improvement in
the waiting list position,

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.760
slight blip in March,
about 18,000 it went up by,

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.930
but I think as you've mentioned,

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.240
we've got some encouraging
management information

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.980
that tells us throughout April

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.680
and throughout May we've
seen some improvements

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.219
in that position, which we will obviously

00:08:07.219 --> 00:08:08.280
report at the end of the month.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.950
So good progress there but we
just need to keep a close eye

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.500
and see the continued
progress throughout the year.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.620
So at aggregate level, our plans,

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:17.730
I know there's an item on the agenda

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.613
that talks about the planning
outputs from this year.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:24.651
We've got plans that add up to 65%

00:08:24.651 --> 00:08:25.590
but it is exceptionally tight

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.720
and I think it's fair
to say that trade off

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.300
between challenging financial position,

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.340
challenging recovery still to do

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.240
is something that's writ large
with the plans that we see.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:37.830
And then what else would I say?

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:41.070
Finally, whilst we need to do more work,

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.968
of course we need to do more work.

00:08:42.968 --> 00:08:46.680
There's also a real need to
transform how we work as well

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.650
and a big focus for us,
I know it's a big focus

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.140
for colleagues up and down the
country, is thinking through

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:55.043
how we transform the model
of care for electives.

00:08:55.043 --> 00:08:57.000
So outpatient has got to
be a significant change

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.810
in terms of how we plan to
deliver that element of care.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:02.250
When you think 80% of patients

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.440
on the waiting list are
waiting for some form

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.760
of outpatient consultation,
diagnostic procedure.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:10.110
So big focus for us

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.120
and we be working with
colleagues up down the country

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.150
to get the right model of care designed.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.343
Should we just touch on cancer quickly?

00:09:19.151 --> 00:09:21.420
Very good progress actually
last year for cancer.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:25.140
So we exited the year, so for March 25

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.060
we had the faster diagnosis standards

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.380
obviously a critical measure

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.700
for us to get a diagnosis more quickly.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.430
So whether someone has a cancer

00:09:34.869 --> 00:09:36.210
or doesn't, really important
we do that really quickly

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:38.460
and it's a key measure
for us to then deliver

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:41.940
the 62 day performance
that we want to see.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:43.980
So what scale of
improvements have we seen?

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:48.540
So against the standard
of 75%, we delivered 79%.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.190
And also the planning guidance ask

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:52.379
was slightly ahead of that.

00:09:52.379 --> 00:09:55.116
We asked for 77% for
that, but we delivered 79.

00:09:55.116 --> 00:09:57.810
So really good progress and
just over one and a half percent

00:09:57.810 --> 00:09:59.193
improvement year on year.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:03.420
For 62 days, again further progress.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:07.320
So we asked, so the standard's 85%,

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:08.670
but we asked in the planning guidance

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.010
for last year to achieve 70%

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:14.010
and we made improvements
so we delivered 71.4,

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.290
which is an improvement
about just over three

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.510
and a half percent year on year.

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.940
So all of that puts us in
a really strong position

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:22.710
for the plans that we have for this year

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:25.800
and we have plans that add up
to the levels of performance

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.200
that we expect to see and asked trust

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.690
and systems to respond to
in the planning guidance.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:32.670
So all of that is positive.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:34.915
Finally, diagnostics, if I could,

00:10:34.915 --> 00:10:38.490
the amount of diagnostic
activity is phenomenal.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:42.453
Well we're now doing
tests, the rate of two

00:10:42.453 --> 00:10:43.620
and a half million tests a month.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:46.440
We've got a 25% increase
in diagnostic activity

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:50.190
between now and if you compare
it to pre pandemic levels

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:53.130
and that to 10% increase year on year.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:55.560
And obviously that is pivotal to make sure

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.270
that we can see patients more quickly,

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.520
diagnose their condition more quickly

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.493
and get their treatment to
them more quickly as well.

00:11:04.014 --> 00:11:05.483
Happy to take any questions, thank you.

00:11:06.843 --> 00:11:10.170
- Okay, Mary, Andrew?

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:11.490
Okay, do you want to go?

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.494
I'll take the questions and then.

00:11:13.494 --> 00:11:14.327
- Thank you very much.

00:11:14.327 --> 00:11:16.920
Firstly, I'm really delighted
to hear the improvement

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:20.370
and recognise the work that's
being done by everybody.

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:23.168
But I've got two questions.

00:11:23.168 --> 00:11:26.850
One is around if we've got a 5% increase

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:31.500
necessary in elective lists

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:34.050
where, not geographically,

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:36.426
but where are the worst performing?

00:11:36.426 --> 00:11:39.870
Are they 12% under or 17% for example?

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:44.580
And my second question is about
the access to diagnostics.

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:48.750
Is that still very patchy
across the whole country

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:51.870
or are we getting to fairer distribution

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:55.143
of some of the sophisticated
machinery that's required?

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:57.780
- Yeah.

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.438
- Let's take the questions
then come back, Andrew.

00:12:00.438 --> 00:12:02.304
- Okay, couple of points from me Mark.

00:12:02.304 --> 00:12:05.730
One is on outpatients, to what extent

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.190
of providers complied with the guidance

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:09.853
that we issued.

00:12:09.853 --> 00:12:10.950
'cause there was a big push last year

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:13.710
on new to follow up rates and clearly

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.120
if we can make progress

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.310
nationally on that, that will move

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:20.430
our kind of performance up
of the scale, which is great.

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:24.387
The other one is on ED,
recognising we've got to do a 3%

00:12:24.387 --> 00:12:29.070
gain this year on top of
any increase in activity.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:32.447
And I'm just working through,
you know, in my mind,

00:12:32.447 --> 00:12:36.580
where have we got plans
to increase capacity,

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.414
'cause if you look at
the 10 worst performers,

00:12:39.414 --> 00:12:42.390
the chances are that in
some of those patches,

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:45.840
there just isn't enough
capacity at this moment in time.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:47.850
So it'd be just to get a view,

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:49.659
be good to get a view on that.

00:12:49.659 --> 00:12:51.192
Recognising we're still at an early part

00:12:51.192 --> 00:12:52.620
of this financial year

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:54.870
and you know, we've got
the winter coming up.

00:12:56.224 --> 00:12:57.921
- Okay, Robert.

00:12:57.921 --> 00:12:58.754
- Sorry to follow up to Mary's question,

00:12:58.754 --> 00:13:02.220
I think, in the diagnostic
performance improvements,

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:04.740
how important have the CDCs been?

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.650
And my recollection is
that we are only part

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:10.567
of the way towards the ambition for CDCs.

00:13:10.567 --> 00:13:12.600
So how do you see that developing

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:14.730
or progressing in the next 12 months?

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:15.563
- And Mark.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:18.210
- So continuing the diagnostics theme

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:21.450
for a second, I mean that rate
of rise is quite phenomenal

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.547
and it's extraordinary in a way

00:13:23.547 --> 00:13:24.784
that we're keeping up with it.

00:13:24.784 --> 00:13:25.770
But I mean that can't carry on forever

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:26.630
and I think it's quite important

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:28.800
to have a fit more granular understanding

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:31.710
of what the nature of
the diagnostic test is.

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:33.464
Is it mainly radiological?

00:13:33.464 --> 00:13:35.520
I think there's some
quite important question

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.485
to understand actually what's going on.

00:13:37.485 --> 00:13:41.897
And I've got a couple of other questions

00:13:41.897 --> 00:13:43.683
as well, on the delayed discharge,

00:13:44.856 --> 00:13:47.782
it's quite important to
have an explanation for that

00:13:47.782 --> 00:13:49.110
because that's the sort
of stock and flow problem.

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:52.990
And so is the problem in getting
people into the community,

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:55.330
where does the cause for that lie

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.360
and a point on children's admissions,

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:01.830
but maybe that's not this item

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:04.963
but are we going to be talking about that

00:14:04.963 --> 00:14:07.021
anywhere else or not?

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.690
If not I'll make the point now,

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:14.690
which is that dental admissions

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:15.960
are one of the communist causes

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.643
of admission to hospital.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:20.970
There is a dental plan coming,

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:22.860
but the critical thing

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:25.740
with young children is
actually dental hygiene.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:28.050
And this is actually
about relatively low cost

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:29.460
interventions in the community.

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:31.260
It's primary prevention.

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:33.450
And it seems to me that at the very least

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:35.839
we ought to understand
the numbers of admissions

00:14:35.839 --> 00:14:38.220
that are due to dental
on an ongoing basis.

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:39.840
And I think it's as critically important

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:42.505
that this is something that
should be quite easy to tackle

00:14:42.505 --> 00:14:46.830
but isn't being, and I've
got one more point actually,

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:49.050
which is that on the
performance bar charts

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:51.865
by trust it's slightly odd

00:14:51.865 --> 00:14:53.670
that there's more bars
than there are texts.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:56.430
And so you see about half the trust

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:59.310
and that seems an odd
way to present the data.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:01.020
And I just comment that at the best end

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:02.940
of the performance chart,

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:05.566
they're the specialist
hospitals by and large.

00:15:05.566 --> 00:15:06.840
So it's quite important
to distinguish them

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:08.880
because they are actually
outliers in terms

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:10.590
of the sort performance curve.

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:11.820
Yeah, so quite a lot of points.

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:15.800
- Okay, so on a presentation
of data, I suggest we come back

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:17.457
to that 'cause I think
that's a wider point

00:15:17.457 --> 00:15:18.512
and we want to pick that up

00:15:18.512 --> 00:15:20.310
when we come onto talking about metrics.

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:23.100
So I think questions for
you Mark, first of all,

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:28.100
extra 5% activity needed
to tackle elective care.

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:30.300
How do you see that happening?

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:32.246
Are there some parts of the country

00:15:32.246 --> 00:15:33.303
that are ahead of others?

00:15:33.303 --> 00:15:35.310
I think that links in
very well to outpatients.

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:37.565
How are we doing on
outpatient transformation?

00:15:37.565 --> 00:15:40.860
Are we capturing some of
the benefits of technology

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:42.554
that's starting to emerge?

00:15:42.554 --> 00:15:45.540
Diagnostics, again,
questions about variation

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.920
in performance across the country.

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:49.800
Question about CDCs

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:52.110
to which I will ask specifically on that.

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:54.600
What utilisation rates have we got?

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:57.600
And more generally what is utilisation

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.720
of diagnostic capacity looking like?

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.540
And is that done over a 60 hour week,

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:04.903
72 hour week, 98 hour week?

00:16:04.903 --> 00:16:07.830
How do we count it and
what's that look like?

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:09.150
And then there's the wider point

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:10.759
that Mark was raising

00:16:10.759 --> 00:16:12.030
about what sort of tests do we do?

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:13.740
And you could add into that,

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.000
how well do we think we're doing

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.072
on ensuring appropriate use of diagnostics

00:16:18.072 --> 00:16:21.154
and then who's going to pick up ED?

00:16:21.154 --> 00:16:22.211
- I'll pick that.

00:16:22.211 --> 00:16:23.044
- You'll pick, okay,
so some questions then

00:16:23.044 --> 00:16:25.770
about overall ED capacity,

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:29.820
overall UEC plan including
delayed discharge,

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:32.400
and I think maybe on the dental one Mark,

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:35.313
I might defer that if that's
okay we come back to 'cause,

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.815
unless Claire wants to come, oh it's yes.

00:16:39.815 --> 00:16:41.880
There's a lot of work happening

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:44.040
on exactly what you
said out in the country

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.740
and certainly I know ICBs
have a very big focus

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:49.590
on tackling prevention
with local authorities

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.660
as well as increasing capacity.

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:55.356
But it's slightly different conversations.

00:16:55.356 --> 00:16:57.691
We'll come back to that,
is that all right Mark?

00:16:57.691 --> 00:16:58.524
- Yeah, very good.

00:16:58.524 --> 00:17:00.000
So in terms of the performance variation,

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.197
we're still seeing quite
a bit of variation.

00:17:04.197 --> 00:17:06.116
You know, and this is in
terms of what the absolute,

00:17:06.116 --> 00:17:09.753
the RTT position looks
like to start the year.

00:17:11.041 --> 00:17:14.222
And we know that some
organisations have had, you know,

00:17:14.222 --> 00:17:15.055
they've got different configuration

00:17:15.055 --> 00:17:15.888
of how they deliver the services.

00:17:15.888 --> 00:17:18.240
So they've had throughout COVID

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:22.034
and afterwards, where they've had a hot

00:17:22.034 --> 00:17:23.480
and cold split, you know,
so they've been able

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:25.050
to protect elective capacity,
quite often they're the ones

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:27.900
who've been able to
safeguard the elective work

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.420
in the immediate periods post pandemic

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:32.340
and others have just
got years of experience

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:35.370
and stronger performance
as a starting point.

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:38.640
So we're trying to work out
how we learn from the best

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:42.180
and spread that learning
across the whole country.

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:45.000
And we are then working with organisations

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:47.220
that are needing more support to drive up

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.530
and get up to the 60% minimum

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.920
that we're expecting throughout this year.

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:53.940
So to do that,

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:56.370
we put organisations
into the tiering process

00:17:56.370 --> 00:17:59.330
that we have as have been in place now

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:00.163
for a couple of years

00:18:00.163 --> 00:18:01.770
and with some good results actually,

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:03.570
what we find are organisations

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:05.845
welcome the additional
support that they get,

00:18:05.845 --> 00:18:07.950
the additional insight that's brought in,

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.230
you know, from the GIRFT team and others

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:11.880
and that has shown

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:14.103
that it drives up performance improvement.

00:18:14.992 --> 00:18:16.590
So that's the model that we are continuing

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.621
to use throughout the year.

00:18:18.621 --> 00:18:22.473
In terms of if we just
go to outpatients then,

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.040
what would I say in particular,

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:29.884
Andrew, point about patient
initiated follow up,

00:18:29.884 --> 00:18:34.348
we know that there's
huge amounts of potential

00:18:34.348 --> 00:18:36.422
if we were able to increase

00:18:36.422 --> 00:18:39.930
the use of patient initiated follow up.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:41.970
It's been pretty stubborn actually

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:44.940
in terms of what we've been
able to deliver so far.

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:47.676
So we're trying to get in
excess of, you know, 5%.

00:18:47.676 --> 00:18:50.790
We've been varying, you know, organisation

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:52.980
to organisation three to 5%.

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.020
But there are some
encouraging signs in different

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:58.710
specialties where we can
see up to 15% improvement

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:01.650
in some specific specialties.

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:05.640
So Professor Tim Briggs
through the GIRFT team,

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:07.290
we've got guidance that's been published

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:09.310
and through the interactions he's having

00:19:10.329 --> 00:19:11.362
with individual organisations,

00:19:11.362 --> 00:19:13.320
we're starting to see some of
the capacity released there.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.480
But we need to do it at greater scale

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:20.010
because the potential is there,
the diagnostic tool is there

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:22.740
to work out which specialties
are going to be best served

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.920
for PIFU and the technology
is there to enable us

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:29.250
to start to deploy this and
have some significant results.

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:32.100
So that's going to be a big
focus for us this year as well

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:33.570
because the potential to release

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:35.400
quite a bit of capacity is there

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.550
and we need to get hold of that this year.

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:40.800
- Mark, sorry, can I ask you on that?

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:42.165
What's the block?

00:19:42.165 --> 00:19:44.970
- So what do I think the block is?

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:47.874
I think it's probably
not the most engaging way

00:19:47.874 --> 00:19:49.110
to focus on the percentage opportunity.

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:50.070
I think what we've got to do

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:52.950
is address two concerns that people have.

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.178
One, if you're a patient,

00:19:55.178 --> 00:19:57.362
a patient potentially thinking
they're going to get lost

00:19:57.362 --> 00:19:58.500
in the system and if I need to come back

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:01.620
and see my clinician, do I have
to start again in the queue?

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:03.690
So that's the first
thing we need to answer

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:06.336
those legitimate concerns
that patients may have.

00:20:06.336 --> 00:20:09.000
And there are legitimate
concerns from clinicians

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.919
that say, well if I need
to see my patient again,

00:20:11.919 --> 00:20:14.550
does it mean they'll have
to start again in the queue?

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:16.830
And of course, what we're not advocating

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:18.390
is either those things to be true,

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:20.460
but we need to have a model
that is resilient enough

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:22.170
that gives patients confidence

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.208
and gives our clinicians confidence.

00:20:24.208 --> 00:20:25.620
And I think that changes the conversation

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:27.840
from the percentage
improvement we need to see

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.570
into how we address safety
concerns and improve access.

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.450
- I think there's a bit
of OD work here to do

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:35.040
with individual clinicians

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:39.090
because when you appoint a clinician,

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:41.070
you don't give them a lot of advice

00:20:41.070 --> 00:20:43.950
on typically in a trust on
how to manage their list.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:44.783
- Yeah.

00:20:45.629 --> 00:20:47.460
- And I think we need to get

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:50.970
clinicians on side here around, you know,

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:54.700
the OD aspects of doing things differently

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:59.220
and I think a lot of people are up for it,

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:01.545
but what we're doing in organisations

00:21:01.545 --> 00:21:03.090
is doing a kind of blanket approach.

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:05.409
This is what you've got to do, you know,

00:21:05.409 --> 00:21:08.100
you must reduce your follow
up ratio to X, Y, and Z,

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.650
rather than, here you
are, here's your work,

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:14.820
you see 5,000 out patients,
you know of which 1000 are new.

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:18.360
How can we tweak that a little
bit to provide better care?

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:19.650
'Cause if you say to a clinician,

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.037
look, you can spend longer
with your new patients

00:21:22.037 --> 00:21:25.440
and have less of a, you know, comeback

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:27.204
with some of your follow ups

00:21:27.204 --> 00:21:29.700
in order to, you know, rebalance that work

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:32.610
and provide a higher quality of service.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:35.190
And it's about how you approach this

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:37.710
I think in organisations
that's the real key

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:39.660
in getting, you know, docs on side,

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:41.887
I think it's absolutely mission critical.

00:21:41.887 --> 00:21:43.590
- Yeah, completely agree.

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:45.420
Clinical leadership in
all this is critical

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:47.340
and we know there's huge amounts

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:49.500
of clinical appetite here to lead the way

00:21:49.500 --> 00:21:52.503
with some of these improvements
that we need to see

00:21:52.503 --> 00:21:53.336
and we're going to need
to maximise on that

00:21:53.336 --> 00:21:56.040
right through the year and
beyond this year as well.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:57.480
In terms of diagnostics,

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.340
CDCs have played a massive role

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:03.510
in terms of having local
access to diagnostic tests.

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:04.890
I think there's more we need to do

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:08.007
about making sure we're making best use

00:22:08.007 --> 00:22:08.840
of the facilities that we have

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:11.930
and co-locating some
outpatient type functions

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:13.440
so they're able to see clinicians

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:15.548
not just have a diagnostic
test in the facilities.

00:22:15.548 --> 00:22:17.307
And I know that up

00:22:17.307 --> 00:22:20.190
and down the country
there's good models in place

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.080
that we can learn from to make best use

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.960
of the investment that's
been put in place.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:26.010
What else would I say?

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.920
So appropriate use is going
to be really important

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:31.650
because we can't, I agree,
we can't just continue

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:33.540
with the rate of increase we're seeing

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:36.210
'cause it's every single year
we're seeing this increase

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:38.850
in diagnostic tests and of
course if patient needs them,

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:40.470
they need to have access.

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:42.790
But again, technology is
going to help us with this

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:44.456
'cause we're not going
to be able to keep up

00:22:44.456 --> 00:22:45.847
with the workforce

00:22:45.847 --> 00:22:47.760
that's going to be
necessary to do the test

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:50.100
and then to do the analysis, the tests,

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:51.870
the reading of reports
and so on and so forth.

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:53.790
So what are we doing?

00:22:53.790 --> 00:22:55.740
There's some work going on
with colleges at the moment

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:59.940
looking at, you know, not
how we restrict the use

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:02.119
and application of tests

00:23:02.119 --> 00:23:03.173
but also how we make sure

00:23:03.173 --> 00:23:05.132
that the right patients are
getting access to the test.

00:23:05.132 --> 00:23:05.965
Because if we have more patients

00:23:05.965 --> 00:23:08.220
just having routine diagnostic tests

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.200
where it's not necessary
to their condition,

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:13.380
it's just in case something is found,

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:15.030
then it potentially disadvantages those

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.190
that do need urgent access to the test.

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:18.900
Which is a careful balance to be struck.

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:20.490
But we're working with
the colleges to make sure

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:23.832
that we've got some guidance
that we can issue to trusts,

00:23:23.832 --> 00:23:27.900
obviously based on clinical
advice, to make sure

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:30.390
that we're making best use of
all the resources that we have

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:32.340
for the patients that need them most.

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:34.320
- Why do we think it needs
more workforce though?

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:38.664
'Cause increasingly we've
got technology that helps us.

00:23:38.664 --> 00:23:41.397
So isn't it more tech
other than more people?

00:23:41.397 --> 00:23:43.410
- I think it's definitely
more tech we need,

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:45.360
I think what we need to do is scale up

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.790
where we've seen the tech work really well

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:50.850
because we've seen, you know,
from a reporting perspective,

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:55.170
some fantastic AI tools being
deployed very early days.

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:56.280
We need to take the learning from that

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:59.220
and start to think through,
how does it get deployed

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:01.800
that will release clinicians

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:04.290
to do the tests rather than having

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:05.700
to do the routine analysis of it.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.410
So some brilliant
results that we're seeing

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:10.740
but not it's not right embedded
across the country yet.

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:12.379
- Vin, did you want to come in on that?

00:24:12.379 --> 00:24:15.563
- Yeah, I think the other bit

00:24:15.563 --> 00:24:17.198
is that there's quite a
lot quite simple technology

00:24:17.198 --> 00:24:18.840
we can use in order to improve things.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:22.200
So we've got technology tools

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.360
that will help clinicians
select the right test

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:27.060
in whether you're doing demand
management, if you're trying

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:29.640
to reduce things like
non-attendance at appointments,

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:32.610
you can use the NHS app in
order to send out notifications

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:35.581
in some places using
SMS messaging and so on.

00:24:35.581 --> 00:24:38.760
There's software that
we've put into MRI scanners

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:39.750
in the last few years

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:42.690
that's accelerated the rate
at which they capture images

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:45.779
by 30%, you know, I can go on and on.

00:24:45.779 --> 00:24:47.220
But actually there's technology
that's already available

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:48.960
before you get to the fancy stuff.

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:50.550
- Yeah, so are we investing enough

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:53.899
in the technology to
enable the improvements?

00:24:53.899 --> 00:24:56.100
- So I think what we need to.

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:57.123
Rhetorical question.

00:24:58.050 --> 00:24:58.923
- Big question.

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:03.240
I think what we need to do
is from the early pilots

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:05.850
that we've seen where there's
some like dramatic results

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:08.867
is quickly work out how
we can get this tech

00:25:08.867 --> 00:25:11.130
greater awareness of the opportunity

00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:12.810
and get it deployed more quickly

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.160
across the whole service

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.695
rather than just being
in a few organisations.

00:25:16.695 --> 00:25:17.790
I think that's the challenge before us,

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:20.579
which we're planning to respond to.

00:25:20.579 --> 00:25:22.740
And then finally the point on utilisation,

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:24.660
I need to come back to
you on data with that.

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:26.760
I think there's definitely opportunity.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:29.447
Some of the data that we
have around, you know,

00:25:29.447 --> 00:25:33.450
for some diagnostic kit
that's in hospitals,

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:35.640
not in the CDCs, we've got
good visibility of that

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:39.033
and the utilisation is
actually generally good.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:41.640
It's more about you know, the downtime

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:43.200
that you have with some older kit

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.974
that can distort sometimes
the utilisation figures

00:25:45.974 --> 00:25:46.807
that we see.

00:25:46.807 --> 00:25:48.846
But I can come back to
you with that Penny.

00:25:48.846 --> 00:25:50.655
- I think that that might
be one that we come back to

00:25:50.655 --> 00:25:52.694
when we think about what
metrics, I think Mark

00:25:52.694 --> 00:25:54.020
just wants to come in on the tech.

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:54.853
- Just on the tech.

00:25:54.853 --> 00:25:56.670
I think both points are pretty clear,

00:25:56.670 --> 00:25:59.790
the actual productivity

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:02.711
and patient outcome variability

00:26:02.711 --> 00:26:07.711
is very wide at the
moment and I think we need

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:10.050
to start defining the
technology investment question

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:14.910
as business process clinician
engagement and technology

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:16.320
and it's the combination of all three

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:19.260
that seems to be the driver of as you say,

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:23.160
when we get that right, some
pretty remarkable outcomes.

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:26.730
- Yeah, okay ED?

00:26:26.730 --> 00:26:30.281
- Yeah, can I just come
back to diagnostics as well.

00:26:30.281 --> 00:26:31.170
Yesterday I was in a CDC yesterday,

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:33.240
six months ago it was an empty shell,

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:36.476
yesterday was absolutely
purring and ready to expand.

00:26:36.476 --> 00:26:39.990
We're thinking about new diagnostics

00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:42.051
'cause they dealt with the original brief

00:26:42.051 --> 00:26:45.420
and on that point it's a
very broad range of things.

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:47.519
So we talk about it as a single thing

00:26:47.519 --> 00:26:48.352
but it's actually very broad.

00:26:48.352 --> 00:26:49.650
The thing they've got into now

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:52.542
in that CDC was much greater need

00:26:52.542 --> 00:26:55.664
for audiology having cracked MRI CT access

00:26:55.664 --> 00:26:58.920
through the CDC and they've
got a lot of room for expansion

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:00.534
and all of that.

00:27:00.534 --> 00:27:02.850
On the tech point, the
biggest frustration yesterday

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:06.750
for staff was two
organisations working together.

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:10.650
Two EPRs, two radiology systems, a dozen

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:14.700
or so booking systems that
had a technology failure

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:18.146
or a power failure a few weeks back.

00:27:18.146 --> 00:27:19.290
And a colleague was telling
me how he had to sit

00:27:19.290 --> 00:27:22.140
with several sheets of
paper with who was booked in

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:24.240
to make phone calls to people.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:26.130
So when I'm thinking
about tech investment,

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:27.510
we really need to think about the basics

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:30.030
that are actually going
to improve people's lives

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:31.080
and functionality.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:32.849
We talk about productivity,

00:27:32.849 --> 00:27:33.682
which we'll do through the day,

00:27:33.682 --> 00:27:35.220
is a technical complicated thing

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:38.334
but it's actually real
life for a lot of people.

00:27:38.334 --> 00:27:39.167
Just the amount of work they've got to do

00:27:39.167 --> 00:27:40.572
for simple tasks is ridiculous.

00:27:40.572 --> 00:27:43.680
But I think there's hope there.

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:47.484
Really good challenge
on the ED thing, Andrew

00:27:47.484 --> 00:27:48.390
and I, you know we're
honest, we're worried

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:50.610
that we're a bit stuck on performance.

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:52.170
We look like we've levelled off.

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:55.050
The things that can give
us a bit of hope there,

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:56.280
I'll come back to the bad thing.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:58.050
There's still roughly two thirds

00:27:58.050 --> 00:28:01.320
of four hour breaches
are not admitted patients

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:04.721
and we're probably never
ever going to be admitted.

00:28:04.721 --> 00:28:07.530
And some organisations
have really cracked up

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:09.750
with better SDEC, UTC access,

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:12.450
hot clinics, CDUs, whatever.

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:16.020
And the urgent care plan
when it's published,

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:18.990
will help start moving people into that

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:21.090
and then we hopefully we deliver all that

00:28:21.090 --> 00:28:23.607
and improve the variation.

00:28:23.607 --> 00:28:25.530
It'll improve access and
performance, decongest,

00:28:25.530 --> 00:28:27.210
but we'll still be left with a bed problem

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:29.910
'cause we don't have
enough beds in our system.

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:33.330
But again, most of our
delays are our problem.

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:36.723
They're not the local
authority system problem.

00:28:36.723 --> 00:28:39.150
They're usually our own community service

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:40.074
quite often run

00:28:40.074 --> 00:28:42.630
by the acute organisation
that has the delays.

00:28:42.630 --> 00:28:44.919
So we've got to really
understand how that works

00:28:44.919 --> 00:28:47.850
I think, and again a colleague
yesterday was telling me

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:50.370
that their biggest problem was drift

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:52.800
in length of stay since COVID.

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:55.080
It was, you know, the
patients are broadly the same

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.977
as staying two or three
days longer than they were.

00:28:57.977 --> 00:28:59.280
No one really quite understands it.

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:01.560
And I think we've got to find
a way of leaning into that.

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:05.430
So it is right that we
need a lot more beds

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:07.500
but there's still a hell
of a lot more we can do

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:08.790
before we get to the point

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:11.190
where that's the only issue to deal with.

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:14.310
- So we've got kind of
three percentage point

00:29:14.310 --> 00:29:16.170
gain to make, haven't we, this year.

00:29:16.170 --> 00:29:19.830
And I'm just wondering
whether we've had enough

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:21.300
of a dialogue with systems

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:25.620
around how they're going to
do that on a system basis.

00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:29.070
'Cause they're all submitting plans.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:31.110
But I dunno whether they're submitting

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:33.360
the effect of those plans.

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:37.860
'Cause often you just
see a kind of, you know,

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:41.910
a neutral position where
the growth gets absorbed,

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:43.050
but there's no betterment

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:47.190
in terms of the four hour
or 12 hour standards.

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:48.960
So it'd be good to see

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:52.770
or good to have sight of
those plans at some point

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:56.040
because we can't go on
just doing a half a percent

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:59.049
or a 1% incremental benefit for people.

00:29:59.049 --> 00:30:01.920
You know, we need to get into the think

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:05.520
of a 5% step every year really

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:06.903
to make a big difference.

00:30:07.977 --> 00:30:10.710
And even if we get to 80%, it's
not going to be a big deal.

00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:13.980
You know, we will still
have very crowded EDs

00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:15.843
and I just feel that, you know,

00:30:17.677 --> 00:30:18.510
we've got to do a lot more

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:19.890
and understand a lot more

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:22.080
around what works in certain settings.

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:23.640
They'll all be different.

00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:25.980
So it requires the local leadership

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:27.570
to actually lead on this

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:29.974
and come up with the local solution.

00:30:29.974 --> 00:30:33.390
But you know, I think our
job is to kind of make sure

00:30:33.390 --> 00:30:36.900
that's happening and I'm not
sure we've got that in place.

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.916
- Well I'll have Jim
come back on that Andrew,

00:30:39.916 --> 00:30:41.010
but can I suggest we come back?

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:45.210
We are going to talk
about future work plan

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:49.277
for the strategy team and I
think this for me is a key one,

00:30:49.277 --> 00:30:52.260
'cause if we keep doing what
we keep doing, as you say,

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:55.471
it's going to be incremental
change when we all recognise

00:30:55.471 --> 00:30:56.880
it needs to be a more
substantial step change.

00:30:56.880 --> 00:30:59.845
So I think let's hold that.

00:30:59.845 --> 00:31:00.916
- Yeah, good.

00:31:00.916 --> 00:31:01.749
- And then that we'll come
back and pick up on that.

00:31:01.749 --> 00:31:04.393
On the immediate though, Jim, when did,

00:31:04.393 --> 00:31:07.170
as you say, a lot of this is operational.

00:31:07.170 --> 00:31:08.070
- Yeah.

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:10.470
- And what operational
support are we giving

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:14.040
to individual trust to help both implement

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:15.270
almost what someone else is doing

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:18.120
but also to accelerate
it as much as possible.

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:20.280
- It's a good point, so
on your point Andrew.

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:22.677
So Sarah-Jane and the team have
been doing that with systems

00:31:22.677 --> 00:31:25.885
and the individual providers.

00:31:25.885 --> 00:31:28.080
So, but we should explain
it more and discussing more,

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:29.580
it's a tricky thing to do.

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:32.850
The organisation I mentioned earlier,

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:34.560
it improved about five or 6%

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:39.150
in ED over the last couple of
months without any extra beds

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:40.920
through targeted improvement effort

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:43.920
facilitated through the
local improvement network.

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:46.710
So it was a good example of something

00:31:46.710 --> 00:31:48.120
that had been kicked off

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:50.490
and then bringing learning from elsewhere

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:52.950
and doing things that we
know work well elsewhere

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:55.800
and trying to adapt them in
your own local circumstance.

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:57.390
What would help with this as well,

00:31:57.390 --> 00:31:59.610
we've made a bit of a
start on transparency

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:01.890
and on your point Mark,
we need to get better

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:05.070
at publishing more data,
showing the variation,

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:08.100
making it easier to access
and interpret as well.

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:10.830
'Cause often the answers there
where you can just find out,

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:13.680
you know, somebody's doing
so much better at X and Y,

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:15.990
you can go and see them
and then and adapt it.

00:32:15.990 --> 00:32:18.600
So we should absolutely
give this some proper time

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:20.100
to talk about, 'cause it's complicated

00:32:20.100 --> 00:32:23.472
and worth a discussion, but
there is stuff going on.

00:32:23.472 --> 00:32:24.529
- Do you want to come?

00:32:24.529 --> 00:32:25.583
- On this point,

00:32:25.583 --> 00:32:26.640
so there's a target
operating model question

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:29.970
about how one improves performance

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:31.230
because there's a lot

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:34.077
of unwarranted variation even within ICBs.

00:32:34.077 --> 00:32:36.390
And so the question is,
is this all going to come

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:38.523
from the centre, shouldn't a lot

00:32:38.523 --> 00:32:40.410
of this improvement be done at ICB level?

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:43.320
And it does come back
I think to real clarity

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:45.510
on what the operating models
at different levels are,

00:32:45.510 --> 00:32:47.730
which frankly I don't think we have yet.

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:49.410
- Yeah, so the whole sort of conversation

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:51.321
has been happening about improvement.

00:32:51.321 --> 00:32:52.154
I'm a bit concerned though about timing

00:32:52.154 --> 00:32:54.120
so I'd quite like to move us
on to financial performance.

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:57.780
So kind of suggest again we
come back to specifically plans

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:00.684
for how we're thinking about improvement.

00:33:00.684 --> 00:33:02.220
'cause that is a big topic.

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:03.420
I just, Claire anything.

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:04.520
- I just wanted to say

00:33:07.316 --> 00:33:10.080
the fact that Jim has said
ultimately we need extra beds

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:12.750
has been really valued by clinical staff

00:33:12.750 --> 00:33:14.970
up and down the country
because for too long,

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:17.123
we've been saying you should be able

00:33:17.123 --> 00:33:17.956
to manage with what you've got.

00:33:17.956 --> 00:33:20.160
I know we can't deliver
them straight away,

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:22.803
but that has been a really good motivator.

00:33:24.105 --> 00:33:27.813
- Yeah, we need to have
a proper bed plan though

00:33:27.813 --> 00:33:28.646
for the next five to 10 years,

00:33:28.646 --> 00:33:30.903
'cause at the moment it's short term.

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:33.926
Claire, anything on dental care?

00:33:33.926 --> 00:33:36.073
- Well you covered it
really Penny, didn't you?

00:33:36.073 --> 00:33:38.580
I mean you know, Mark's
absolutely spot on.

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:40.080
The highest number of
children's admissions

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:42.960
are related to teeth extraction

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:47.220
and most of that should be
prevented by primary prevention.

00:33:47.220 --> 00:33:50.642
I think it's longer than a
ten second answer isn't it?

00:33:50.642 --> 00:33:51.475
I think we should come back to it,

00:33:51.475 --> 00:33:53.160
there's a lot of work going on.

00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:55.143
- Okay, Elizabeth finances.

00:33:56.005 --> 00:33:57.039
- Thank you, yep.

00:33:57.039 --> 00:33:58.920
So I'm going to be covering

00:33:58.920 --> 00:34:00.540
the performance update as at month one

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:04.370
but also finally closing down the 25,

00:34:04.370 --> 00:34:07.088
26 operating plan process.

00:34:07.088 --> 00:34:10.098
I'll take the papers
together, I'll tell you a bit

00:34:10.098 --> 00:34:12.300
of story if that's okay with folks.

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:16.208
So we started the year in January 25

00:34:16.208 --> 00:34:17.662
where we set out the planning guidance.

00:34:17.662 --> 00:34:18.753
So that's priorities

00:34:18.753 --> 00:34:20.880
and planning guidance
for the NHS. It marked

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:23.460
the beginning of this national, local

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:26.370
and regional effort to
align all of our ambitions,

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:28.743
our resources and our
delivery around the plan.

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:33.356
But as you know, it
hasn't been an easy road.

00:34:33.356 --> 00:34:35.640
By the end of February, systems came back

00:34:35.640 --> 00:34:37.473
with quite a troubled position,

00:34:38.713 --> 00:34:40.590
even after including 2.2 billion pounds

00:34:40.590 --> 00:34:42.033
worth of deficit support.

00:34:43.137 --> 00:34:45.720
We were facing a 4.4
billion pound financial gap.

00:34:45.720 --> 00:34:47.640
And on top of this, looking at the plans,

00:34:47.640 --> 00:34:50.700
there was limited confidence
in what had been submitted,

00:34:50.700 --> 00:34:53.280
the quality of the plans
and the deliverability.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:57.030
So in March you will be
aware we had a major reset.

00:34:57.030 --> 00:35:01.473
It involved a leadership
conversation with Penny and Jim.

00:35:02.550 --> 00:35:04.470
I'll always remember the meeting.

00:35:04.470 --> 00:35:07.470
It was very, very honest about
the scale of the challenge

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:09.930
that's financially, operationally

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:11.370
and going back to some of the comments

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:13.740
from our colleagues today.

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:17.190
Also from a cultural perspective,

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:20.643
this is very much OD work as
well as about the numbers.

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:23.460
So we asked systems to go back really

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:26.070
and really ask them to
challenge their assumptions,

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:29.343
tighten their delivery
plans, improve their grip.

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:33.443
Jim was really clear, to
have a sustainable future

00:35:33.443 --> 00:35:36.060
we needed to act now and
live within our means.

00:35:36.060 --> 00:35:39.180
There was, and there is no more money,

00:35:39.180 --> 00:35:41.490
I can't reemphasize that enough.

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:42.720
So it was a bit of a call to arms

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:46.110
and I'm really pleased to
say that we saw clinicians,

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:48.030
chief executives, finance directors

00:35:48.030 --> 00:35:49.893
to name but a few really step up.

00:35:50.730 --> 00:35:52.500
It seems to be a change in mentality

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:55.029
in terms of the approach of planning,

00:35:55.029 --> 00:35:56.100
which we really welcome

00:35:56.100 --> 00:35:57.840
moving away from this deficit
mindset that we've had

00:35:57.840 --> 00:35:59.710
for too long to be honest.

00:35:59.710 --> 00:36:02.130
And the answers are very much out there

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:04.740
in the service which we
will support and enable.

00:36:04.740 --> 00:36:06.810
But as the conversation we've just had

00:36:06.810 --> 00:36:09.887
that Andrew has prompted around UEC,

00:36:09.887 --> 00:36:11.955
you know there'll be some
good practise elsewhere

00:36:11.955 --> 00:36:13.500
there will be able to
share across the country.

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:15.030
But ultimately, and there's a couple

00:36:15.030 --> 00:36:16.710
of case studies I'd like to refer to

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:20.790
for the UEC improvements,
particularly in Plymouth,

00:36:20.790 --> 00:36:23.190
which we've talked around
this table for too long

00:36:23.190 --> 00:36:24.840
for the wrong reasons.

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:26.280
We have seen step change

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:29.370
and it's about leadership
as much as in some places

00:36:29.370 --> 00:36:31.947
a little bit of extra capacity Mary,

00:36:31.947 --> 00:36:35.550
but not so much necessarily
about new models of care

00:36:35.550 --> 00:36:38.455
and really, really strong
clinical leadership.

00:36:38.455 --> 00:36:41.370
So it's been an immense work.

00:36:41.370 --> 00:36:44.640
We had regional teams stepping
in with hands-on support.

00:36:44.640 --> 00:36:46.320
We met with teams,

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:48.480
talked about some really
tricky, wicked issues

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:50.190
and faced those challenges together

00:36:50.190 --> 00:36:52.200
and I'm pleased to say
at the end of April,

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:55.710
the result was we now have
at least a balanced plan.

00:36:55.710 --> 00:36:57.600
The planning gap is closed.

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:00.180
That doesn't mean that
the deficit's gone away.

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:02.700
Obviously we've got a bridge to this

00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:04.650
in that 2.2 billion pounds

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:06.600
worth of deficit support is still there

00:37:07.590 --> 00:37:09.886
but some of our money
would've actually gone

00:37:09.886 --> 00:37:10.740
into allocations.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:13.043
So we're going to resolve
that going forward.

00:37:13.043 --> 00:37:15.030
So it feels like a big step forward.

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:16.830
I wish that was the end of the story

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:17.910
and the plans were there

00:37:17.910 --> 00:37:19.950
and we could be confident on delivery.

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:22.860
But this is a big ask, it's
a really tough ask this year

00:37:22.860 --> 00:37:25.490
and Mark's alluded to some of that.

00:37:25.490 --> 00:37:28.283
So 7.1% efficiency savings required

00:37:28.283 --> 00:37:33.273
a reported 2% reduction
in total workforce.

00:37:34.110 --> 00:37:36.250
Also recognising we know

00:37:37.119 --> 00:37:39.190
that in some plans people
have put in vacancies

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:40.080
they probably won't look to recruit.

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:42.369
So there's some grossing up in there

00:37:42.369 --> 00:37:43.495
that we historically have had.

00:37:43.495 --> 00:37:44.580
So we'll get to the bottom of that

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:47.280
and in aggregate, a 40% reduction

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:49.140
in agency staff by next March.

00:37:49.140 --> 00:37:51.810
So all of this was at the same time,

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:54.123
continuing to recover on electives,

00:37:54.123 --> 00:37:56.880
we're improving emergency performance,

00:37:56.880 --> 00:38:00.713
we are expanding access to
primary care and mental health.

00:38:00.713 --> 00:38:01.830
So huge, huge effort

00:38:01.830 --> 00:38:04.803
and a big thanks to the
service actually for this.

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:09.646
I think on elective

00:38:09.646 --> 00:38:10.710
and some of the performance indicators,

00:38:10.710 --> 00:38:12.870
mark's probably picked
it up so it's aligned

00:38:12.870 --> 00:38:15.009
to the plans in the interest of time.

00:38:15.009 --> 00:38:20.009
We are also seeing then I
suppose just to reference Claire,

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:23.790
further work and plans in place

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:25.830
to actually meet the rising needs

00:38:25.830 --> 00:38:27.780
in primary care and mental health

00:38:27.780 --> 00:38:30.216
in the report we've referenced

00:38:30.216 --> 00:38:33.060
700,000 extra dental appointments,

00:38:33.060 --> 00:38:35.970
urgent dental appointments
as part of the plan

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:39.783
recognising again, you
know, the points just made,

00:38:40.620 --> 00:38:42.540
reducing hospital stays in mental health,

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:46.380
adult mental health units expanding CYP

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:48.240
mental health access

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:51.030
and cutting inpatient reliance
for learning disabilities

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:53.382
and autism by 10%.

00:38:53.382 --> 00:38:54.510
So that's the ambition in the plan

00:38:54.510 --> 00:38:57.390
and just some of the
highlights, during the process,

00:38:57.390 --> 00:38:59.900
we also worked extensively with 12

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:03.240
of our most challenged
systems, in February,

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:05.070
every one of them was planning a deficit.

00:39:05.070 --> 00:39:08.010
I'm pleased to say as of today,
we've now got balanced plans

00:39:08.010 --> 00:39:09.720
and also within the report,

00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:12.510
you can see the same
is true on performance.

00:39:12.510 --> 00:39:17.510
Not everyone is planning to deliver

00:39:17.716 --> 00:39:18.990
its performance targets

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:20.670
as we've set out in the planning guidance.

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:22.710
But that work continues.

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:26.310
So it's not the end of the
process in that respect.

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:28.710
But what's really important
now I think is we move swiftly

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:31.980
on to delivery and we pause,
you know, we've got the plans,

00:39:31.980 --> 00:39:34.710
we've got confidence,
they've been board approved,

00:39:34.710 --> 00:39:36.210
we've got confidence in the leadership

00:39:36.210 --> 00:39:38.910
and the leadership compact
that we now have in place

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:40.260
and we work with those systems

00:39:40.260 --> 00:39:42.210
to create the conditions for success.

00:39:42.210 --> 00:39:44.417
Recognising we are not out there

00:39:44.417 --> 00:39:47.790
on the front line knowing the intricacies

00:39:47.790 --> 00:39:49.950
of what's going on in local organisations.

00:39:49.950 --> 00:39:53.430
But we will be sensitive and curious

00:39:53.430 --> 00:39:55.200
and really work with our organisations

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:56.823
to deliver this year.

00:39:58.505 --> 00:39:59.338
- Okay, thank you Elizabeth.

00:39:59.338 --> 00:40:01.650
So just thought, if
anyone's got any questions,

00:40:01.650 --> 00:40:04.110
conscious so we haven't
really spoken about quality,

00:40:04.110 --> 00:40:08.250
so just wondered whether
Steve, Meghana, Duncan, Claire,

00:40:08.250 --> 00:40:10.023
anything you would like to add?

00:40:11.160 --> 00:40:12.643
- Thank you Penny.

00:40:12.643 --> 00:40:16.020
I think the reference to
7.1% efficiency savings,

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:18.301
which is a tall order.

00:40:18.301 --> 00:40:23.301
We have created a policy for
quality impact assessments

00:40:23.550 --> 00:40:25.540
that chief medical officers

00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:28.080
and chief nursing
officers have to sign off.

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:31.350
We've also provided a practical
tool for all organisations

00:40:31.350 --> 00:40:33.300
that they can use, that way we'll have

00:40:33.300 --> 00:40:34.920
a standardised template

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:38.910
and people when they do the
quality impact assessments,

00:40:38.910 --> 00:40:40.510
there will be of a similar level

00:40:41.468 --> 00:40:42.600
that they can escalate to their boards.

00:40:42.600 --> 00:40:45.120
So really every board in their policy,

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:47.520
it's very clear that every
board should have sight

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:49.020
of these quality impact assessments,

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:51.300
especially if the risk scores are high.

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:53.790
So it's a decision that
they will jointly make

00:40:53.790 --> 00:40:56.373
as unitary boards whether
they go ahead or not.

00:40:57.480 --> 00:40:58.913
- That's reassuring to know.

00:40:58.913 --> 00:41:02.610
I guess that's almost
saying there's a risk

00:41:02.610 --> 00:41:05.109
that if we are focusing on money,

00:41:05.109 --> 00:41:06.377
we've got to check the quality.

00:41:06.377 --> 00:41:08.370
Yeah but there's also improving quality

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:10.844
should improve productivity as well.

00:41:10.844 --> 00:41:14.045
So how are people looking
at that side of the coin?

00:41:14.045 --> 00:41:17.656
- So I think the local, so there are,

00:41:17.656 --> 00:41:19.572
almost all organisations have a

00:41:19.572 --> 00:41:21.022
quality improvement programme

00:41:21.022 --> 00:41:24.000
and I think there's
plenty evidence out there

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:27.432
which says good quality
care costs less, you know,

00:41:27.432 --> 00:41:28.265
and there are many organisations

00:41:28.265 --> 00:41:31.230
who have learned from
systems internationally,

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:33.506
for example Virginia Mason Institute.

00:41:33.506 --> 00:41:37.670
And there's an approach where
we either let 1,000 flowers

00:41:37.670 --> 00:41:39.210
bloom where every clinician

00:41:39.210 --> 00:41:41.850
or non-clinician undertakes
quality improvement projects.

00:41:41.850 --> 00:41:44.250
But really it's about
creating a pull effect

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:46.800
where if there are strategic objectives,

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:49.260
which obviously will be
these operational standards

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:52.410
and how to meet them, then
we create a whole channel

00:41:52.410 --> 00:41:54.780
of everybody who wants to
undertake quality improvement

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:58.230
does so in support of
that one main objective.

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:01.110
And I think if organisations
try to do that,

00:42:01.110 --> 00:42:02.580
essentially you improve quality

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:05.430
and you get the productivity
gain and engagement

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:07.560
and leadership right from the front

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:09.850
and the top of the organisation
is the key in all that.

00:42:09.850 --> 00:42:12.990
- Okay, and Claire, any
comments on my favourite topic

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:15.330
of high levels of variation

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:18.534
in primary care
performance, GP performance?

00:42:18.534 --> 00:42:20.824
- Well as we always say,

00:42:20.824 --> 00:42:21.866
the joy in the curse, isn't it?

00:42:21.866 --> 00:42:22.699
Is the variation across primary care.

00:42:22.699 --> 00:42:25.080
But we are doing work with the ICBs,

00:42:25.080 --> 00:42:26.820
looking at how we can improve

00:42:26.820 --> 00:42:28.110
the management base of the contract

00:42:28.110 --> 00:42:32.040
but also about the
variation between practises

00:42:32.040 --> 00:42:33.150
and across PCN.

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:35.601
So again, it's the more
we can look at the data,

00:42:35.601 --> 00:42:36.817
the better that will be.

00:42:36.817 --> 00:42:39.715
So it's different, just to
add to Meghana's comments,

00:42:39.715 --> 00:42:40.548
when we met with the
most challenged systems,

00:42:40.548 --> 00:42:43.410
I think it's important to
note that Meghana, myself

00:42:43.410 --> 00:42:45.390
and Duncan were present
for those conversations.

00:42:45.390 --> 00:42:46.444
It wasn't a conversation

00:42:46.444 --> 00:42:47.905
entirely through a financial lens.

00:42:47.905 --> 00:42:50.520
It was very much through a quality lens

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:52.200
and a lot of the conversation

00:42:52.200 --> 00:42:54.570
was about the clinical leadership capacity

00:42:54.570 --> 00:42:56.519
within those challenge systems.

00:42:56.519 --> 00:42:58.854
- Duncan did you want to come in?

00:42:58.854 --> 00:43:00.630
- Well just to reflect
back, we are continuing

00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:02.580
to develop the quality strategy

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:04.200
and I'm think we've probably got an update

00:43:04.200 --> 00:43:08.730
going back to the quality
committee in due course

00:43:08.730 --> 00:43:10.140
and I think just important to reflect

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:13.133
that part of that is about
the value for money value

00:43:13.133 --> 00:43:16.830
and how we increase
the visibility on that.

00:43:16.830 --> 00:43:18.390
I guess just a couple of
other things just to add

00:43:18.390 --> 00:43:21.630
that we are doing clearly
in Jim's day one letter

00:43:21.630 --> 00:43:25.170
out to the system, we are
supporting our local systems

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:27.090
with corporate nursing reviews

00:43:27.090 --> 00:43:28.470
and making sure that we've got

00:43:28.470 --> 00:43:31.080
that clinical facing workforce

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:32.742
working with patients

00:43:32.742 --> 00:43:36.570
and making sure that we've
got the right approach to that

00:43:36.570 --> 00:43:40.323
and looking at variation
across those areas as well.

00:43:41.610 --> 00:43:42.997
- Steve.

00:43:42.997 --> 00:43:45.114
- And just to remind the board,

00:43:45.114 --> 00:43:45.947
the National Quality Board

00:43:45.947 --> 00:43:47.310
published this weaker position statement

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:49.700
on the need to remaining
focused on quality

00:43:49.700 --> 00:43:51.330
in the times of financial challenge

00:43:51.330 --> 00:43:53.670
and there's a number of prior publications

00:43:53.670 --> 00:43:55.080
that the NQB has made,

00:43:55.080 --> 00:43:58.203
particularly to help ICBs
in how they address that.

00:43:59.623 --> 00:44:00.456
- Okay Mark.

00:44:00.456 --> 00:44:02.690
- Can I just check that
the in year impacts

00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:05.996
of the pay rises are in these figures.

00:44:05.996 --> 00:44:08.013
- The in year figures, yes.

00:44:09.182 --> 00:44:12.531
So effectively, within the
assumptions at the start

00:44:12.531 --> 00:44:16.857
of the plan we were planning
for a 2.8% in pay award.

00:44:16.857 --> 00:44:18.901
The pay award has been higher.

00:44:18.901 --> 00:44:22.740
In conversations with
the Department of Health,

00:44:22.740 --> 00:44:24.750
we are clear and the
departments are clear,

00:44:24.750 --> 00:44:26.820
we will not be cutting frontline services

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:29.250
in order to afford that wage award.

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:30.990
So we are currently in discussions

00:44:30.990 --> 00:44:35.049
where expecting that uplift to be funded

00:44:35.049 --> 00:44:39.701
and we will ensure that that is allocated

00:44:39.701 --> 00:44:40.651
out to the systems.

00:44:41.668 --> 00:44:42.720
- I think that's helpful.

00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:43.775
And can I ask another question

00:44:43.775 --> 00:44:45.198
which I think you'll
probably want to postpone,

00:44:45.198 --> 00:44:46.200
but the way the figures are presented

00:44:46.200 --> 00:44:48.980
are very much through in
the tables at the end,

00:44:48.980 --> 00:44:50.850
the lenses of the acute trust,

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:53.370
we're talking about a
shift in commissioning,

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:55.680
which will mean money
moving out of the trust

00:44:55.680 --> 00:44:57.900
and into the community.

00:44:57.900 --> 00:44:59.940
That's going to be quite challenging

00:44:59.940 --> 00:45:01.680
and I suspect we need a longer

00:45:01.680 --> 00:45:03.630
and separate conversation about that.

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:06.330
- So we'll pick that up when
we have a longer conversation

00:45:06.330 --> 00:45:08.702
later today on the 10 year plan.

00:45:08.702 --> 00:45:09.990
So I suggest we don't go into that again.

00:45:09.990 --> 00:45:12.417
I would say I think we need to do more

00:45:12.417 --> 00:45:13.250
about how we present our data

00:45:13.250 --> 00:45:14.520
so we're looking more holistically

00:45:14.520 --> 00:45:17.130
and looking at both the
commissioner provider lens.

00:45:17.130 --> 00:45:18.590
I agree, so we'll come back to that.

00:45:18.590 --> 00:45:20.369
So we're going to run out of time.

00:45:20.369 --> 00:45:21.427
We have too many questions.

00:45:21.427 --> 00:45:23.580
So I've got Jane, Andrew, Mark.

00:45:23.580 --> 00:45:27.098
- Well thank you and and
actually mine very much falls

00:45:27.098 --> 00:45:28.200
on from Mark's last point.

00:45:28.200 --> 00:45:29.033
So happy to sort of
put it down as a marker

00:45:29.033 --> 00:45:30.000
for the later discussion.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.920
'Cause I was just interested
to know when you went back

00:45:31.920 --> 00:45:34.782
to systems and essentially said, you know,

00:45:34.782 --> 00:45:36.570
come on, need to see different figures

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:39.450
and change how many of them
were looking at the shift left

00:45:39.450 --> 00:45:42.600
and were looking to pick up
Mark's earlier example at dental

00:45:42.600 --> 00:45:45.840
and how much a relatively
modest investment, you know,

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:47.370
outside of the acute sector

00:45:47.370 --> 00:45:49.890
could really benefit sort
of, you know, the flow.

00:45:49.890 --> 00:45:51.000
So I just put that down

00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:53.641
as a marker Penny for
the later discussion.

00:45:53.641 --> 00:45:57.123
- And again, I think we
come back to that, Mark.

00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:00.497
- Question for Jim.

00:46:00.497 --> 00:46:02.160
So if you take a step back
from all these numbers,

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:05.066
7% efficiency improvement,
2% headcount reduction,

00:46:05.066 --> 00:46:10.066
improvement in quality, in
all operational turnarounds,

00:46:11.400 --> 00:46:13.050
when you look back down the mountain

00:46:13.050 --> 00:46:16.620
after it's been done, the
route was actually quite clear,

00:46:16.620 --> 00:46:18.725
when you start at the
beginning of the mountain,

00:46:18.725 --> 00:46:22.050
generally you're met with disbelief.

00:46:22.050 --> 00:46:24.765
And actually I was just
wondering about the storytelling

00:46:24.765 --> 00:46:28.890
and the cultural journey
you are going through

00:46:28.890 --> 00:46:30.840
to give people the belief.

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:34.290
'Cause I think we all believe
it's entirely possible.

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:36.657
I don't think you'd get the same response

00:46:36.657 --> 00:46:38.356
if you actually spoke
to people on the ground

00:46:38.356 --> 00:46:39.933
and interesting how you're approaching it.

00:46:39.933 --> 00:46:40.766
- Very good question.

00:46:40.766 --> 00:46:42.660
Well let let me just
take Andrew's question.

00:46:42.660 --> 00:46:45.000
- So mine was just to remind everyone,

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:50.000
you know, we are behind by
110, right at month one.

00:46:50.130 --> 00:46:53.490
So what's the process right

00:46:53.490 --> 00:46:56.052
in in helping organisations deliver

00:46:56.052 --> 00:46:59.070
if they start to go off track?

00:46:59.070 --> 00:47:00.330
Because I think it's really important

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:02.400
that we're clear about that.

00:47:02.400 --> 00:47:04.605
- Yeah and those quite related.

00:47:04.605 --> 00:47:05.649
John, take those Jim.

00:47:05.649 --> 00:47:06.720
- Yeah, I think the storytelling questions

00:47:06.720 --> 00:47:08.280
are really fantastic points.

00:47:08.280 --> 00:47:11.040
So the first bit was trying to explain

00:47:11.040 --> 00:47:13.698
and have a, an honest conversation about,

00:47:13.698 --> 00:47:16.490
let's all accept where we are and then all

00:47:16.490 --> 00:47:18.000
of us in different settings

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:20.160
finding absolutely every opportunity

00:47:20.160 --> 00:47:22.080
to have those conversations.

00:47:22.080 --> 00:47:25.110
National, regional,
smaller groups of people.

00:47:25.110 --> 00:47:27.641
For example, I'm going to Bolton next week

00:47:27.641 --> 00:47:29.912
to meet a bunch of leaders on Tuesday,

00:47:29.912 --> 00:47:32.670
wherever we can do that,

00:47:32.670 --> 00:47:35.490
especially with clinical
and operational people,

00:47:35.490 --> 00:47:38.670
have the conversation about
the reality of where we are,

00:47:38.670 --> 00:47:41.820
what's possible, what's
being done elsewhere.

00:47:41.820 --> 00:47:43.770
Try and help condition people

00:47:43.770 --> 00:47:46.710
to think about what's
possible, what's not possible.

00:47:46.710 --> 00:47:49.797
But it's absolutely, it's
going to be an every day

00:47:49.797 --> 00:47:52.380
of the week activity for a very long time.

00:47:52.380 --> 00:47:54.060
And then, you know,
hopefully we'll catch on

00:47:54.060 --> 00:47:56.970
with enough momentum, enough
self generate momentum

00:47:56.970 --> 00:47:59.550
where it starts to propel itself.

00:47:59.550 --> 00:48:02.368
The things Elizabeth said, the first bit

00:48:02.368 --> 00:48:03.201
we're looking for was a response

00:48:03.201 --> 00:48:06.030
and not a rejection of
what we needed to do.

00:48:06.030 --> 00:48:07.315
That was great.

00:48:07.315 --> 00:48:09.442
There were actually people
stayed in the room that day

00:48:09.442 --> 00:48:12.270
to solve their problems,
which was fantastic to see.

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:13.910
You can't obviously solve it

00:48:13.910 --> 00:48:15.600
in you know, really practical sense

00:48:15.600 --> 00:48:18.040
but the will to crunch through

00:48:19.125 --> 00:48:20.340
and as you said, you know, confront things

00:48:20.340 --> 00:48:24.600
that they knew were issues and
had felt unable to deal with

00:48:24.600 --> 00:48:26.430
or not allowed to deal with.

00:48:26.430 --> 00:48:28.950
Although some other kind
of odd barrier in the way.

00:48:28.950 --> 00:48:31.271
So we've got through that bit I think.

00:48:31.271 --> 00:48:34.290
But this is going to be, you know,

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:37.440
every step of the next year.

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:41.226
And then beyond just trying
to keep making sense of it,

00:48:41.226 --> 00:48:42.510
there'll be other bumps in
the road as Andrew said,

00:48:42.510 --> 00:48:44.940
can't just dribble through the air

00:48:44.940 --> 00:48:46.650
with a bit of an overspend.

00:48:46.650 --> 00:48:49.376
So we're following up with
the organization's plan,

00:48:49.376 --> 00:48:52.110
systems off plan, we'll
do some board to boards

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:55.590
around specific variation, so CHC,

00:48:55.590 --> 00:48:57.540
whether there are commissioning issues,

00:48:58.488 --> 00:49:01.260
whether specific workforce
issues, you know, et cetera.

00:49:01.260 --> 00:49:04.778
But again, as being mentioned
a few times this morning,

00:49:04.778 --> 00:49:06.481
we need to bring insights to that.

00:49:06.481 --> 00:49:08.190
We need to show the data,
we need to be able to show

00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:11.604
what's possible, what is being delivered

00:49:11.604 --> 00:49:12.930
somewhere else to help you know,

00:49:12.930 --> 00:49:15.697
build that confidence that it's possible.

00:49:15.697 --> 00:49:16.920
- Yeah, I wonder whether
Rob you could help

00:49:16.920 --> 00:49:20.610
in developing a bit of
a narrative including,

00:49:20.610 --> 00:49:22.350
not to discuss now, but take it offline,

00:49:22.350 --> 00:49:23.793
developing that narrative.

00:49:24.776 --> 00:49:26.370
'cause as Jim said, well I think everyone

00:49:26.370 --> 00:49:28.917
around the room's doing
a lot of going out there,

00:49:28.917 --> 00:49:30.780
but it might be good to have
something that we could,

00:49:30.780 --> 00:49:33.390
to Mark's point actually
share a bit more clearly.

00:49:33.390 --> 00:49:34.950
- Certainly, and just briefly,

00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:37.020
we've already developed

00:49:37.020 --> 00:49:39.478
I think quite a plan around all of that.

00:49:39.478 --> 00:49:41.662
So I'll share that for
comment and discussion.

00:49:41.662 --> 00:49:42.840
After today's meeting.

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:43.673
- Thank you.

00:49:43.673 --> 00:49:46.847
Okay, so Miranda, do you want to update us

00:49:46.847 --> 00:49:49.320
on the performance assessment
framework consultation?

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:50.862
- Yeah, thank you.

00:49:50.862 --> 00:49:52.917
I just wanted to give
an update to the board

00:49:52.917 --> 00:49:53.910
of where we are on the
consultation process

00:49:53.910 --> 00:49:56.696
for the performance assessment framework.

00:49:56.696 --> 00:49:58.857
So colleagues may remember back in March

00:49:58.857 --> 00:50:02.430
the current draft framework
was issued for a period

00:50:02.430 --> 00:50:03.807
of testing and engagement

00:50:03.807 --> 00:50:05.993
and with recognition of a consultation

00:50:05.993 --> 00:50:08.833
before it's finalised.

00:50:08.833 --> 00:50:12.030
We're currently in the
consultation period at the moment.

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:14.473
The consultation finishes tomorrow.

00:50:14.473 --> 00:50:16.770
I think there's one point
I wanted to highlight.

00:50:16.770 --> 00:50:19.980
So early feedback from
the testing engagement

00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:24.540
since March has suggested
it's quite complex

00:50:24.540 --> 00:50:26.820
and there is a very broad set of metrics

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:30.480
and could it risk the focus for this year.

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:32.070
I mean the conversations we've just had

00:50:32.070 --> 00:50:35.610
in terms of the importance
of delivering on quality,

00:50:35.610 --> 00:50:38.584
the operational priorities
and the finances.

00:50:38.584 --> 00:50:43.410
So as part of the consultation
we've asked the question of,

00:50:43.410 --> 00:50:47.370
you know, given the focus for
this year, would it be helpful

00:50:47.370 --> 00:50:51.600
to shorten the set of
metrics to allow that focus,

00:50:51.600 --> 00:50:53.670
but a full recognition that we will want

00:50:53.670 --> 00:50:55.503
to review the metrics following

00:50:55.503 --> 00:50:58.141
the release of the 10 year plan.

00:50:58.141 --> 00:50:59.555
We'll focus on prevention

00:50:59.555 --> 00:51:00.600
that, you know, Claire's talked about.

00:51:00.600 --> 00:51:01.740
In the shorten this,

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:04.230
I did engage with Duncan, Claire

00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:07.850
and Meghana to make sure
we've retain, you know,

00:51:07.850 --> 00:51:08.940
sufficient metrics on quality.

00:51:08.940 --> 00:51:12.345
We've got the operating priority set out

00:51:12.345 --> 00:51:14.940
and the planning, guidance
and finance as a question.

00:51:14.940 --> 00:51:17.919
So that's where we are
on the consultation.

00:51:17.919 --> 00:51:21.840
It finishes tomorrow, we will
be consolidating the responses

00:51:21.840 --> 00:51:23.640
for a fast turnaround

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:26.515
because we have a commitment
to release the framework

00:51:26.515 --> 00:51:27.348
for the 1st of July.

00:51:28.194 --> 00:51:29.249
- Okay, thank you Miranda.

00:51:29.249 --> 00:51:31.980
And I think for some
reassurance I've gone off

00:51:31.980 --> 00:51:33.960
with Miranda's help and
done a bit of a tally

00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:35.337
of where the metrics are

00:51:35.337 --> 00:51:39.300
and actually, we are very
focused on quality metrics

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:40.530
and access metrics

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:43.200
but that's actually the bulk
of what we are looking at

00:51:43.200 --> 00:51:45.206
is quality and access.

00:51:45.206 --> 00:51:47.915
So I think that gives some reassurance.

00:51:47.915 --> 00:51:49.269
I still think there's more work to do

00:51:49.269 --> 00:51:50.102
and we'll come back to that later.

00:51:51.044 --> 00:51:53.161
Okay, any other questions Miranda?

00:51:53.161 --> 00:51:54.240
No, no.

00:51:54.240 --> 00:51:55.920
Okay, and then Duncan,

00:51:55.920 --> 00:51:59.820
we've got a specific paper
that we need to agree.

00:51:59.820 --> 00:52:00.990
- Yeah, thanks Penny.

00:52:00.990 --> 00:52:02.730
Just I'll be brief

00:52:02.730 --> 00:52:04.768
'cause I think you've
got the report in there

00:52:04.768 --> 00:52:05.820
and you can take it as read

00:52:05.820 --> 00:52:09.135
and the exec has approved this already,

00:52:09.135 --> 00:52:09.968
but this is a legal duty

00:52:09.968 --> 00:52:13.435
that we have to abide by every year.

00:52:13.435 --> 00:52:14.850
But I think it also just
sets out how important

00:52:14.850 --> 00:52:18.570
core equalities duties are
across all of the work we do.

00:52:18.570 --> 00:52:20.100
So this brings together

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:21.720
the work across the whole organisation

00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:24.086
and across a number of
different directors.

00:52:24.086 --> 00:52:27.030
I think it's fair to say in the report

00:52:27.030 --> 00:52:29.280
you'll see there's been
progress that's been made,

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:31.950
but there are clearly areas
that need more progress.

00:52:31.950 --> 00:52:33.806
I will call one of them out,

00:52:33.806 --> 00:52:36.104
which is maternity and
maternal inequalities

00:52:36.104 --> 00:52:39.690
and perinatal mortality,
particularly for women

00:52:39.690 --> 00:52:43.620
and babies who are black,
Asian or mixed ethnicity.

00:52:43.620 --> 00:52:46.509
And we've got to go
further and faster on that.

00:52:46.509 --> 00:52:48.186
And I know that audit will come back

00:52:48.186 --> 00:52:50.243
to this board particularly on maternity

00:52:50.243 --> 00:52:51.510
I think in the coming meetings.

00:52:51.510 --> 00:52:53.076
But I did want to flag that

00:52:53.076 --> 00:52:55.110
because I think it is really stark

00:52:55.110 --> 00:52:57.431
and talks to the work that we need to do

00:52:57.431 --> 00:52:58.770
in terms of resetting the pace

00:52:58.770 --> 00:53:01.650
of change in some of these areas.

00:53:01.650 --> 00:53:03.603
The other thing I would just call out

00:53:03.603 --> 00:53:06.714
is clearly with the changes to ICBs

00:53:06.714 --> 00:53:09.566
and the changes to NHS
England coming together

00:53:09.566 --> 00:53:10.664
with the Department of Health,

00:53:10.664 --> 00:53:13.500
we have updated the objectives
to reflect those changes

00:53:13.500 --> 00:53:16.226
and we will clearly work
over the next period of time

00:53:16.226 --> 00:53:20.280
with the Department of Health
on bringing these together

00:53:20.280 --> 00:53:23.010
with them and also mindful
of the ICB changes,

00:53:23.010 --> 00:53:24.450
making sure that they reflect that.

00:53:24.450 --> 00:53:26.670
So I probably didn't want to
say anything more than that,

00:53:26.670 --> 00:53:28.410
but I'm happy to take
questions or comments

00:53:28.410 --> 00:53:30.723
and obviously for the board to approve.

00:53:33.088 --> 00:53:35.181
- Okay, thank you.

00:53:35.181 --> 00:53:36.014
I think you've got approval

00:53:36.014 --> 00:53:38.580
and as you say, we need
to keep coming back to it.

00:53:38.580 --> 00:53:40.290
So that nicely links us on.

00:53:40.290 --> 00:53:43.579
So we've been talking
about current performance,

00:53:43.579 --> 00:53:46.800
now we're going to look
forward to future strategy.

00:53:46.800 --> 00:53:49.890
So I think Tom, we're going
to give a high level overview

00:53:49.890 --> 00:53:51.690
of both 10 year plan

00:53:51.690 --> 00:53:55.263
and future work programme
for the strategy team,

00:53:55.263 --> 00:53:58.385
which again, in the executive,

00:53:58.385 --> 00:53:59.454
non-executive conversation

00:53:59.454 --> 00:54:01.207
we're going to have later
today, we'll get into this

00:54:01.207 --> 00:54:03.300
in a bit more detail, but
we're obviously constrained

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:05.713
in terms of how much we
can say publicly ahead

00:54:05.713 --> 00:54:07.607
of the plan being published.

00:54:07.607 --> 00:54:09.608
- Thanks Penny.

00:54:09.608 --> 00:54:11.005
So we're close to the end

00:54:11.005 --> 00:54:12.868
of what's been a year long process

00:54:12.868 --> 00:54:14.974
and as you say, it's pretty limited

00:54:14.974 --> 00:54:17.130
in what could be said
because we're at that point

00:54:17.130 --> 00:54:19.873
where the work that's been
done is in the process

00:54:19.873 --> 00:54:21.420
of becoming agreed government policy,

00:54:21.420 --> 00:54:24.644
but it's not quite at that point yet.

00:54:24.644 --> 00:54:27.210
I think it's fair to say that the themes

00:54:27.210 --> 00:54:30.000
will be familiar to
people, the three shifts.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:33.540
So from hospital to
community, analogue to digital

00:54:33.540 --> 00:54:35.730
and treatment to prevention.

00:54:35.730 --> 00:54:37.620
And then the kind of key enablers

00:54:37.620 --> 00:54:41.070
that are required to deliver those shifts.

00:54:41.070 --> 00:54:42.060
In a couple of weeks time,

00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:44.569
we're going to have the spending review

00:54:44.569 --> 00:54:45.704
which will set the overall framework,

00:54:45.704 --> 00:54:47.512
the overall financial framework

00:54:47.512 --> 00:54:50.190
for the service over the next three years.

00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:53.010
And obviously in a tight
fiscal environment,

00:54:53.010 --> 00:54:55.950
that will obviously determine the pace

00:54:55.950 --> 00:54:59.760
of implementation of the 10 year plan.

00:54:59.760 --> 00:55:01.350
I think next week we're expecting

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:02.790
that it will go into the process

00:55:02.790 --> 00:55:05.569
of cross government agreement,

00:55:05.569 --> 00:55:07.590
which is also known as right round.

00:55:07.590 --> 00:55:10.410
That's where it's sent to
other government departments

00:55:10.410 --> 00:55:15.180
where they get to comment
and input into the process

00:55:15.180 --> 00:55:16.710
before a final decision's taken

00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:20.940
at the domestic policy
subcommittee at the cabinet.

00:55:20.940 --> 00:55:24.120
Obviously there'll need to
be appropriate delivery plans

00:55:24.120 --> 00:55:28.307
put in place in order to
take forward the commitments

00:55:28.307 --> 00:55:30.210
that are made in the plan

00:55:30.210 --> 00:55:33.269
and that's something that
we'll all be collectively

00:55:33.269 --> 00:55:36.543
working on in the weeks and months ahead.

00:55:37.823 --> 00:55:39.551
I think that's probably
as much as I can say

00:55:39.551 --> 00:55:43.050
on where we're at with the
10 year plan at this point.

00:55:43.050 --> 00:55:45.933
And then on the kind of
strategy work programme,

00:55:46.860 --> 00:55:48.210
we're starting to try and define

00:55:48.210 --> 00:55:50.430
what that work programme looks like.

00:55:50.430 --> 00:55:53.790
In many respects, you can think of it

00:55:53.790 --> 00:55:56.643
over a kind of multiple years.

00:55:56.643 --> 00:55:59.400
Some of it will be what
you might think about

00:55:59.400 --> 00:56:01.110
as sort of operational strategy.

00:56:01.110 --> 00:56:05.310
So how do you translate the
ambitions from the 10 year plan

00:56:05.310 --> 00:56:08.010
into the kind of practical
delivery strategies

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:10.290
that solve the real
problems for the service

00:56:10.290 --> 00:56:13.620
so that they can take
forward those commitments.

00:56:13.620 --> 00:56:16.170
There's certain areas of
kind of thematic strategy.

00:56:16.170 --> 00:56:18.840
So for instance, let's
say prevention or quality

00:56:18.840 --> 00:56:20.850
or mental health.

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:22.380
The third sort of category of strategy

00:56:22.380 --> 00:56:26.283
that we're thinking about you
might term wicked problems.

00:56:27.240 --> 00:56:28.073
So kind of targeted work

00:56:28.073 --> 00:56:32.280
solving seemingly intractable problems.

00:56:32.280 --> 00:56:35.010
And then finally there are
kind of cross government themes

00:56:35.010 --> 00:56:38.850
and that's where the NHS
is part of the story.

00:56:38.850 --> 00:56:41.970
So it's one of the actors,
but it's not the only actor.

00:56:41.970 --> 00:56:44.010
So a good example of that might be work

00:56:44.010 --> 00:56:47.649
and health where the NHS
clearly has a big role to play,

00:56:47.649 --> 00:56:51.150
but it's not the only
actor in that category.

00:56:51.150 --> 00:56:54.630
And so those are the kind
of different categories

00:56:54.630 --> 00:56:56.007
that we're thinking about

00:56:56.007 --> 00:57:00.480
in terms of the sort of
forward programme for strategy.

00:57:00.480 --> 00:57:02.130
But you know, the kind of chief

00:57:02.130 --> 00:57:05.910
amongst all of those is
translating the commitments

00:57:05.910 --> 00:57:08.130
and ambitions in the 10 year plan

00:57:08.130 --> 00:57:13.020
into real delivery
strategies for the service.

00:57:13.020 --> 00:57:13.853
I stop there.

00:57:16.117 --> 00:57:17.910
- And in terms of how we agree

00:57:19.260 --> 00:57:21.508
what those different programmes are,

00:57:21.508 --> 00:57:26.043
how would you like to
involve the board in that?

00:57:27.758 --> 00:57:29.730
- I think we're going to start
that process this afternoon

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:32.100
and start to, I think it's a process

00:57:33.110 --> 00:57:34.980
of the board coming to a consensus

00:57:34.980 --> 00:57:36.330
about what needs to be done

00:57:36.330 --> 00:57:39.081
by listening to what
the service has to say.

00:57:39.081 --> 00:57:40.740
'cause the primary purpose

00:57:40.740 --> 00:57:42.210
is to solve those problems

00:57:42.210 --> 00:57:45.663
that only NHS England
nationally can solve.

00:57:46.740 --> 00:57:49.530
There's then also a
process of working closely

00:57:49.530 --> 00:57:52.150
with DHSC colleagues

00:57:53.194 --> 00:57:55.920
and with ministers to determine
what their priorities are

00:57:55.920 --> 00:57:57.690
and therefore the sequence.

00:57:57.690 --> 00:57:58.820
I think what we want to get to

00:57:58.820 --> 00:58:01.260
is a kind of what's the long list

00:58:01.260 --> 00:58:05.009
and then prioritise from that long list,

00:58:05.009 --> 00:58:07.230
starting with, you know,
what are the commitments

00:58:07.230 --> 00:58:09.720
that have been made in the
10 year plan for delivery.

00:58:09.720 --> 00:58:12.870
And then thinking about the
other themes that are priorities

00:58:12.870 --> 00:58:16.083
as expressed by the board
and also by ministers.

00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:20.580
- Thanks Tom, that's very helpful.

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:22.140
We tend to look at productivity

00:58:22.140 --> 00:58:24.210
as a rather internal activity,

00:58:24.210 --> 00:58:27.450
but actually if we keep
more children in school,

00:58:27.450 --> 00:58:29.070
that's good for education.

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:31.560
If we get more people back into work,

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:34.020
that has enormous economic impact

00:58:34.020 --> 00:58:35.580
and there's not been much discussion

00:58:35.580 --> 00:58:37.200
about the sort of economics

00:58:37.200 --> 00:58:39.390
of the life sciences strategy as well,

00:58:39.390 --> 00:58:41.550
which is also critically important.

00:58:41.550 --> 00:58:42.895
- Yeah,

00:58:42.895 --> 00:58:43.830
- So I just think we've got

00:58:43.830 --> 00:58:46.110
to look at this question of productivity

00:58:46.110 --> 00:58:48.240
in a much broader sense actually.

00:58:48.240 --> 00:58:52.380
- Yeah, so let me maybe make,
actually that's a good point.

00:58:52.380 --> 00:58:55.113
I did meant to bring that
up on the last agenda item.

00:58:56.130 --> 00:58:58.350
Some of those things
again will be picked up

00:58:58.350 --> 00:58:59.640
when we come to the 10 year plan.

00:58:59.640 --> 00:59:02.550
You make a good point, which
we do keep trying to say

00:59:02.550 --> 00:59:04.126
but we keep forgetting

00:59:04.126 --> 00:59:04.959
is that we do have the industrial strategy

00:59:04.959 --> 00:59:07.006
which includes a big section

00:59:07.006 --> 00:59:08.640
on life sciences strategy coming out,

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:10.320
I think less colleagues

00:59:10.320 --> 00:59:12.150
around the same time as the 10 year plan.

00:59:12.150 --> 00:59:14.760
Yeah. So obviously there's
a key thing to make sure

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:16.537
that they are reasonably aligned.

00:59:16.537 --> 00:59:18.150
Otherwise that would look a bit odd.

00:59:18.150 --> 00:59:21.223
So Vin, you might want
to come back in on that

00:59:21.223 --> 00:59:22.663
and then on productivity.

00:59:22.663 --> 00:59:24.810
So Elizabeth is leading
quite a lot of work on that.

00:59:24.810 --> 00:59:26.823
It's absolutely fundamental.

00:59:26.823 --> 00:59:28.245
I think it relates a bit to the narrative

00:59:28.245 --> 00:59:29.767
we spoke about earlier,
but I think we need

00:59:29.767 --> 00:59:33.169
a much more tangible,
that's not the right word.

00:59:33.169 --> 00:59:37.118
More substantive discussion
on that in this forum.

00:59:37.118 --> 00:59:39.175
So I'll bring that back, yeah.

00:59:39.175 --> 00:59:40.939
Okay, Vin you want to come in?

00:59:40.939 --> 00:59:43.410
- Yeah, two things, thanks chair.

00:59:43.410 --> 00:59:44.580
So firstly just on the question

00:59:44.580 --> 00:59:48.870
of the industrial strategy
in the life sciences sector,

00:59:48.870 --> 00:59:51.979
I've chaired the expert working group.

00:59:51.979 --> 00:59:53.636
We put an expert working group together

00:59:53.636 --> 00:59:55.800
that both informed the
life sciences sector plan

00:59:55.800 --> 00:59:59.370
for the industrial strategy
and the innovation research

00:59:59.370 --> 01:00:02.160
and life sciences sector
of the 10 year health plan.

01:00:02.160 --> 01:00:05.760
So in the process we should
have alignment between the two,

01:00:05.760 --> 01:00:07.440
although of course the industrial strategy

01:00:07.440 --> 01:00:09.240
will speak relatively speaking

01:00:09.240 --> 01:00:12.060
more to industry and to economic growth.

01:00:12.060 --> 01:00:14.310
Whereas a 10 year health
plan will speak much more

01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:17.550
to patients, the public and to the NHS.

01:00:17.550 --> 01:00:18.660
And we've done a great deal of work

01:00:18.660 --> 01:00:20.860
to ensure there's
alignment between the two.

01:00:22.094 --> 01:00:23.138
I think the second point

01:00:23.138 --> 01:00:23.971
is that we're obviously looking forward

01:00:23.971 --> 01:00:27.630
to the spending review and
the 10 year health plan,

01:00:27.630 --> 01:00:31.140
but some of the investments
in it will be significant

01:00:31.140 --> 01:00:32.940
and of course we will therefore

01:00:32.940 --> 01:00:35.400
need to have our teams
writing high quality

01:00:35.400 --> 01:00:38.430
green book treasury business cases.

01:00:38.430 --> 01:00:40.980
Certainly what I'm doing with my team

01:00:40.980 --> 01:00:43.556
and I know colleagues will
be doing this round table,

01:00:43.556 --> 01:00:44.850
is to make sure that in this year,

01:00:44.850 --> 01:00:46.740
we are absolutely geared up

01:00:46.740 --> 01:00:50.520
to get those business cases
written to high quality

01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:52.230
through approvals right to treasury

01:00:52.230 --> 01:00:55.923
so we can hit the ground
running in April next year.

01:00:57.700 --> 01:01:00.153
- Great, any other questions for Tom?

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:04.511
Okay, all right, move on.

01:01:04.511 --> 01:01:07.053
So Miranda, back to you
for ICB operating model.

01:01:07.950 --> 01:01:09.290
- Okay, thanks Penny.

01:01:09.290 --> 01:01:11.640
So I wanted to give an
update on where we are

01:01:11.640 --> 01:01:13.440
with the model ICB blueprint.

01:01:13.440 --> 01:01:15.060
I'll take the paper as read.

01:01:15.060 --> 01:01:18.180
I think there's three points
that I wanted to stress

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:20.552
before the conversation.

01:01:20.552 --> 01:01:21.385
So I think it's really important

01:01:21.385 --> 01:01:23.700
to thank the system
leaders who've helped us

01:01:23.700 --> 01:01:27.150
and with a very enthusiastic
and thoughtful approach.

01:01:27.150 --> 01:01:29.520
And in terms of designing
the blueprint rapidly

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:32.470
over the last few weeks,
I did want to appoint,

01:01:32.470 --> 01:01:35.070
I think we all have this huge sense

01:01:35.070 --> 01:01:37.850
that this impacts thousands of people

01:01:37.850 --> 01:01:38.683
who are currently doing their jobs

01:01:38.683 --> 01:01:40.694
to the best of their abilities.

01:01:40.694 --> 01:01:42.694
And this represents a significant change

01:01:43.623 --> 01:01:44.850
and therefore there's a real sense

01:01:44.850 --> 01:01:47.655
of responsibility from all of us,

01:01:47.655 --> 01:01:50.356
including within NHS England,
the Department of Health,

01:01:50.356 --> 01:01:52.556
that we need to give
certainty to colleagues

01:01:53.490 --> 01:01:55.110
as soon as we can,

01:01:55.110 --> 01:01:57.060
which is why we've done
this process so rapidly

01:01:57.060 --> 01:02:01.020
to start setting those guardrails in place

01:02:01.020 --> 01:02:02.820
to support colleagues as we manage

01:02:02.820 --> 01:02:04.320
through this change process

01:02:04.320 --> 01:02:07.112
and co-production has been
really, really important

01:02:07.112 --> 01:02:08.183
as part of that.

01:02:08.183 --> 01:02:09.455
And I think it'd be quite helpful, Rob,

01:02:09.455 --> 01:02:10.830
if you just sort of talk
about our engagement approach

01:02:10.830 --> 01:02:14.879
and how we changed it to sort
of help us deliver at pace

01:02:14.879 --> 01:02:16.470
before I go into my next point.

01:02:16.470 --> 01:02:18.033
- Certainly happy to chair.

01:02:18.988 --> 01:02:21.388
I think Jim mentioned at
the top of the meeting,

01:02:22.443 --> 01:02:23.506
we need to pivot

01:02:23.506 --> 01:02:26.700
into a much more
collaborative way of working

01:02:26.700 --> 01:02:29.602
and even Mark your points on storytelling

01:02:29.602 --> 01:02:31.908
I think feed into this
early start that we've had

01:02:31.908 --> 01:02:34.600
with the development of the ICB blueprint

01:02:35.650 --> 01:02:37.552
by bringing together a bunch of leaders,

01:02:37.552 --> 01:02:40.288
CEOs, chairs, but also some strategy leads

01:02:40.288 --> 01:02:44.345
or the folks together to
co-create this blueprint

01:02:44.345 --> 01:02:47.204
really rapidly, as you say, through April.

01:02:47.204 --> 01:02:50.920
And I think that the result
is in the draught blueprint

01:02:50.920 --> 01:02:52.410
that we've got, it's got
so much more additional

01:02:52.410 --> 01:02:54.840
both credibility, but actually,

01:02:54.840 --> 01:02:56.370
because it's the ICB leadership

01:02:56.370 --> 01:02:58.950
that's narrating this
back out to the system,

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:01.560
there's quite a bit of
energy around all of this,

01:03:01.560 --> 01:03:03.510
which you might not have predicted

01:03:03.510 --> 01:03:07.970
at the point in which the
day one letter went out.

01:03:07.970 --> 01:03:09.420
And my guess is given the
scale of the challenge

01:03:09.420 --> 01:03:11.040
that folks are facing,

01:03:11.040 --> 01:03:13.637
that energy is going
to be really important

01:03:13.637 --> 01:03:15.520
in getting us to the
place we need to get to

01:03:15.520 --> 01:03:17.073
by October the end of the year.

01:03:18.240 --> 01:03:20.318
- Thanks Rob, so that leads
into the second point.

01:03:20.318 --> 01:03:21.870
So a real sense of ambition
that's driving this work,

01:03:21.870 --> 01:03:25.505
which we felt, I think Penny

01:03:25.505 --> 01:03:27.900
and Jim as we've been developing this work

01:03:27.900 --> 01:03:29.760
and a real sense of the role

01:03:29.760 --> 01:03:33.600
of strategic commissioners

01:03:33.600 --> 01:03:37.446
in terms of what they're
doing to commission service.

01:03:37.446 --> 01:03:38.310
Not only meeting the population,

01:03:38.310 --> 01:03:40.770
but really improving population health.

01:03:40.770 --> 01:03:42.600
And I think that's a compelling vision

01:03:42.600 --> 01:03:45.603
for the staff in ICBs going forward.

01:03:46.500 --> 01:03:49.590
And fundamentally, the draft document

01:03:49.590 --> 01:03:54.570
is about creating the conditions
to help ICBs to succeed

01:03:54.570 --> 01:03:56.520
and deliver the maximum possible value

01:03:56.520 --> 01:04:00.450
in those improvements for patients

01:04:00.450 --> 01:04:03.690
and for the taxpayers, of course it sits,

01:04:03.690 --> 01:04:05.610
as the paper sets there
are independencies,

01:04:05.610 --> 01:04:09.562
it sits amongst the wider
work that we are doing

01:04:09.562 --> 01:04:11.250
in terms of our operating
model to look at the role

01:04:11.250 --> 01:04:13.110
of the region and the centre.

01:04:13.110 --> 01:04:15.390
We started early with ICBs

01:04:15.390 --> 01:04:19.080
and you know, we need to
make sure it continues

01:04:19.080 --> 01:04:22.110
to align, to get towards a transparent,

01:04:22.110 --> 01:04:25.680
rules based approach about
how we all work together

01:04:25.680 --> 01:04:27.647
to deliver on the priorities.

01:04:27.647 --> 01:04:29.520
We're all clear on our
roles and accountabilities

01:04:29.520 --> 01:04:32.160
and I do think ultimately simplifying

01:04:32.160 --> 01:04:33.729
the system architecture,

01:04:33.729 --> 01:04:36.810
getting our operating model right,

01:04:36.810 --> 01:04:39.720
the financial flows and incentives
working in the right way

01:04:39.720 --> 01:04:42.600
and everyone being clear on accountability

01:04:42.600 --> 01:04:45.540
will help us with the foundations

01:04:45.540 --> 01:04:48.913
to deliver on the ambitions
of the 10 year health plan.

01:04:48.913 --> 01:04:50.577
So that's what we're trying to achieve

01:04:50.577 --> 01:04:51.900
from the blueprint.

01:04:51.900 --> 01:04:54.420
The third and important point is,

01:04:54.420 --> 01:04:56.610
we know we are not there yet.

01:04:56.610 --> 01:04:59.250
It is a draft document we've recognised

01:04:59.250 --> 01:05:02.460
as we sent this out it
needs further development

01:05:02.460 --> 01:05:06.438
and engagement which
we are continuing to do

01:05:06.438 --> 01:05:11.438
and I think that you'll see from the paper

01:05:11.647 --> 01:05:13.920
that plans are due in
at the end of tomorrow.

01:05:13.920 --> 01:05:16.260
And I think that gives
us the first opportunity

01:05:16.260 --> 01:05:19.770
to see how the plans are coming together

01:05:19.770 --> 01:05:23.610
in terms of delivering the £18.76 per head

01:05:23.610 --> 01:05:27.574
running cost envelope.

01:05:27.574 --> 01:05:29.280
But also importantly an opportunity for us

01:05:29.280 --> 01:05:31.140
to look at those plans

01:05:31.140 --> 01:05:32.700
and start to pick out the areas

01:05:32.700 --> 01:05:35.250
of good practise which can support

01:05:35.250 --> 01:05:38.130
and help colleagues in terms
of developing their plans.

01:05:38.130 --> 01:05:41.141
And I know from speaking
to the ICB leaders

01:05:41.141 --> 01:05:46.141
as we were developing,
they're really keen to share

01:05:46.740 --> 01:05:48.879
like, I've solved this issue,

01:05:48.879 --> 01:05:50.793
let's put some of these ideas up here

01:05:50.793 --> 01:05:51.780
so we can all support

01:05:51.780 --> 01:05:55.260
of pace of change that's needed.

01:05:55.260 --> 01:05:57.570
So I think that sort of hopefully covers

01:05:57.570 --> 01:05:59.760
the key points I wanted to say,

01:05:59.760 --> 01:06:01.980
but happy to take any questions.

01:06:01.980 --> 01:06:03.000
- Okay, lets take questions.

01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:06.000
So I've got Mark and then Andrew.

01:06:06.000 --> 01:06:06.983
- So thank you very much.

01:06:06.983 --> 01:06:09.492
It is a really important piece of work.

01:06:09.492 --> 01:06:13.260
The current document has an
awful lot of aspiration in it

01:06:13.260 --> 01:06:15.543
and isn't really a target operating model.

01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:18.360
I suppose my question is about capacity

01:06:18.360 --> 01:06:19.530
because there's an enormous amount

01:06:19.530 --> 01:06:23.319
of talk in it about
local actuarial analysis.

01:06:23.319 --> 01:06:24.152
There's just an enormous amount

01:06:24.152 --> 01:06:26.903
of analytical capability needed

01:06:26.903 --> 01:06:29.940
and I just worry about
whether the capacity

01:06:29.940 --> 01:06:32.970
is going to exist for every ICB,

01:06:32.970 --> 01:06:36.363
whatever the final structure
is to be able to do that.

01:06:37.640 --> 01:06:39.450
And I also think that at the
moment there is a mismatch

01:06:39.450 --> 01:06:42.180
in the document between
the responsibilities

01:06:42.180 --> 01:06:43.650
and the accountabilities actually.

01:06:43.650 --> 01:06:46.350
And so getting the balance
between, it's quite difficult

01:06:46.350 --> 01:06:49.650
for an ICB to be responsible for something

01:06:49.650 --> 01:06:54.510
and then the accountability
at the regional level.

01:06:54.510 --> 01:06:56.850
And so I think getting that
relationship, you know,

01:06:56.850 --> 01:06:58.500
doing one bit of it
without doing the others

01:06:58.500 --> 01:07:00.632
is quite problematic actually.

01:07:00.632 --> 01:07:04.740
And what's happened is
that ICPs are being asked

01:07:04.740 --> 01:07:08.550
to cut very substantially,
which I can see is necessary

01:07:08.550 --> 01:07:10.440
but quite difficult to do that
if you haven't got a target

01:07:10.440 --> 01:07:13.863
operating model that you are
making your changes against.

01:07:14.700 --> 01:07:17.146
So the danger is, you cut the wrong people

01:07:17.146 --> 01:07:18.450
by accident as it were.

01:07:18.450 --> 01:07:20.760
So again, we're not going to
resolve it in this meeting

01:07:20.760 --> 01:07:22.943
I don't think, but
there's a lot to discuss.

01:07:24.112 --> 01:07:24.945
- Okay, well let's take
the other questions

01:07:24.945 --> 01:07:28.192
and I'll come back to
some of that. Andrew.

01:07:28.192 --> 01:07:31.230
- Thank you, so Miranda,
my point's around risk

01:07:31.230 --> 01:07:33.210
with this, so we are reducing

01:07:33.210 --> 01:07:36.633
the number of ICBs potentially,

01:07:38.201 --> 01:07:42.400
we've also got the 50%
reduction to work through

01:07:43.768 --> 01:07:45.060
and yet you are doing the piece of work

01:07:45.060 --> 01:07:47.680
that should, you know,
give you a bottom line

01:07:48.621 --> 01:07:50.221
in terms of saving potentially

01:07:50.221 --> 01:07:51.330
once you've done the modelling

01:07:51.330 --> 01:07:52.563
that you've described.

01:07:53.520 --> 01:07:58.505
My challenge is if someone
had said to me 18 months ago

01:07:58.505 --> 01:08:00.810
that we'd be taking 50% out of ICB costs

01:08:00.810 --> 01:08:04.677
plus 50% out of corporate costs,

01:08:04.677 --> 01:08:06.330
I'd have thought

01:08:06.330 --> 01:08:09.090
that's a bit too much
if I'm absolutely honest

01:08:09.090 --> 01:08:12.828
and I just wonder about the, you know,

01:08:12.828 --> 01:08:16.091
why we've hit on 50 rather than 30 or 20

01:08:16.091 --> 01:08:18.690
and I do recognise

01:08:18.690 --> 01:08:23.310
that we are looking at a
pre COVID set of expenditure

01:08:23.310 --> 01:08:28.200
but I think the health service
has changed a lot since 18/19

01:08:28.200 --> 01:08:30.720
and if you look at, you know,
some of the quality issues

01:08:30.720 --> 01:08:32.520
that we're trying to address

01:08:32.520 --> 01:08:35.790
that does impact on management
capacity and the cost base.

01:08:35.790 --> 01:08:40.680
So I would welcome the opportunity

01:08:40.680 --> 01:08:44.910
to see the work and see
what drops out of that

01:08:44.910 --> 01:08:46.830
and match it against the 50%

01:08:46.830 --> 01:08:49.750
rather than we're chasing the 50%

01:08:49.750 --> 01:08:50.747
for the sake of doing it.

01:08:50.747 --> 01:08:54.600
And it does impact on trusts
as well that, you know,

01:08:54.600 --> 01:08:56.850
we're talking about literally thousands

01:08:56.850 --> 01:09:00.630
of people being shed as
part of this process.

01:09:00.630 --> 01:09:03.179
So I think it'd be good to see

01:09:03.179 --> 01:09:04.012
that work once you've done it

01:09:04.012 --> 01:09:06.870
and just convince ourselves that the 50%

01:09:06.870 --> 01:09:08.430
is the right number.

01:09:08.430 --> 01:09:10.620
'Cause it may well not be.

01:09:10.620 --> 01:09:11.730
- Okay, Duncan?

01:09:11.730 --> 01:09:14.430
- Yeah, firstly, can I
just say how grateful I am

01:09:14.430 --> 01:09:15.677
to the way Miranda

01:09:16.633 --> 01:09:18.505
and her colleagues have
really leaned into this

01:09:18.505 --> 01:09:20.642
with our clinical community
and the CNOs and the CMOs

01:09:20.642 --> 01:09:23.880
and also thank them for
their input during this time.

01:09:23.880 --> 01:09:26.758
I guess mine is risk based as well,

01:09:26.758 --> 01:09:28.680
but I'm probably asking myself a question

01:09:28.680 --> 01:09:30.916
my medical director colleagues,

01:09:30.916 --> 01:09:32.172
but I think it's important,

01:09:32.172 --> 01:09:34.889
given there is a
significant amount of change

01:09:34.889 --> 01:09:36.723
and the statutory
responsibilities that are held

01:09:36.723 --> 01:09:39.000
by us at NHS England but also within ICBs

01:09:39.000 --> 01:09:41.973
and thinking about things
like safeguarding for example.

01:09:42.960 --> 01:09:46.498
I guess is there anything
else we need to be doing

01:09:46.498 --> 01:09:48.970
to make sure through
our governance systems

01:09:49.837 --> 01:09:52.437
that we have got proper
line of sight on those risks

01:09:53.540 --> 01:09:54.373
and any additional interventions

01:09:54.373 --> 01:09:56.883
given there's such a pace of change.

01:09:58.676 --> 01:10:00.293
- Okay, did you want
to come in now Jim or.

01:10:01.219 --> 01:10:02.052
- I'll come in at the end.

01:10:02.052 --> 01:10:03.210
- Yeah, that'd be very good.

01:10:03.210 --> 01:10:04.533
Okay, Mary take that.

01:10:05.704 --> 01:10:07.687
- This may be one for this afternoon

01:10:07.687 --> 01:10:10.606
or a later meeting but when
I looked at point eight

01:10:10.606 --> 01:10:12.270
about working with local
government associations

01:10:12.270 --> 01:10:14.670
with continuing healthcare et cetera,

01:10:14.670 --> 01:10:17.730
no mention is made of
housing in this paper

01:10:17.730 --> 01:10:22.680
and the only way we moved mental
health forward 30 years ago

01:10:22.680 --> 01:10:25.740
was to have housing to move people into

01:10:25.740 --> 01:10:29.787
and I think that is a huge issue for ICBs

01:10:29.787 --> 01:10:32.490
and local communities
if we're going to reduce

01:10:32.490 --> 01:10:35.229
length of stay in inpatient.

01:10:35.229 --> 01:10:36.993
So I just want to kind of raise it

01:10:36.993 --> 01:10:40.568
as something we shouldn't
lose sight of. Robert.

01:10:40.568 --> 01:10:41.920
- So yeah, I was going to make

01:10:42.917 --> 01:10:45.120
the same sort of point Mark
was in terms of capability

01:10:45.120 --> 01:10:46.680
because the strategic commissioning

01:10:46.680 --> 01:10:50.070
is so crucial I would
assume, to delivering

01:10:50.070 --> 01:10:52.020
what we're going to
see in the 10 year plan

01:10:52.020 --> 01:10:53.767
and the left shift

01:10:53.767 --> 01:10:54.600
and all that and just reassurance

01:10:54.600 --> 01:10:56.100
that that capability is there

01:10:56.100 --> 01:10:57.270
when these cuts are being made.

01:10:57.270 --> 01:10:59.429
But a very specific question,

01:10:59.429 --> 01:11:01.770
there's no mention of research here.

01:11:01.770 --> 01:11:04.290
There was another paper I
saw on ICB operating model

01:11:04.290 --> 01:11:06.510
that talked about shifting
responsibility of research

01:11:06.510 --> 01:11:10.410
to regions away from
ICBs, which surprised me

01:11:10.410 --> 01:11:12.543
'cause I would've thought that back

01:11:12.543 --> 01:11:14.292
to the life sciences agenda

01:11:14.292 --> 01:11:15.270
and the importance of research

01:11:15.270 --> 01:11:18.903
and innovation to delivering
the right future, apologies,

01:11:20.460 --> 01:11:22.050
then I would've thought research

01:11:22.050 --> 01:11:24.183
ought to be the responsibility of ICBs.

01:11:24.183 --> 01:11:27.870
- Okay, so Miranda, first of all,

01:11:27.870 --> 01:11:30.900
questions around ICB
capability and capacity

01:11:30.900 --> 01:11:35.031
and some of that will link
into the integration work,

01:11:35.031 --> 01:11:36.825
which we'll come onto in a minute.

01:11:36.825 --> 01:11:39.450
So what's the centre need to
do to support building that?

01:11:39.450 --> 01:11:41.340
But that is a big question.

01:11:41.340 --> 01:11:42.640
They're all big questions.

01:11:43.769 --> 01:11:46.380
The second is really
pushing on relative roles,

01:11:46.380 --> 01:11:48.360
accountabilities, target operating model,

01:11:48.360 --> 01:11:51.840
how that works across regions and ICBs

01:11:51.840 --> 01:11:53.010
and how we are going to, we know

01:11:53.010 --> 01:11:55.166
that's a big question people have

01:11:55.166 --> 01:11:56.229
and how we're going to make that clearer.

01:11:56.229 --> 01:11:58.405
And I think that's where we
pick up where do functions

01:11:58.405 --> 01:12:01.080
such as safeguarding go,
where does research go?

01:12:01.080 --> 01:12:03.180
How are we going to work all that through?

01:12:03.180 --> 01:12:07.530
And then the third big
question is all about risk.

01:12:07.530 --> 01:12:10.740
We are moving a lot of different
things at the same time.

01:12:10.740 --> 01:12:12.390
How are we assuring ourselves

01:12:12.390 --> 01:12:15.690
that we are keeping a good
eye out on those risks?

01:12:15.690 --> 01:12:17.580
And within that is very explicitly

01:12:17.580 --> 01:12:21.570
around the costs and the 18 pounds 76.

01:12:21.570 --> 01:12:24.600
And then the final bit is
how do we assure ourselves

01:12:24.600 --> 01:12:26.970
that the new ICBs will work effectively

01:12:26.970 --> 01:12:29.703
with local authorities and wider partners?

01:12:32.010 --> 01:12:34.050
- Brilliant, okay, I'll kick off.

01:12:34.050 --> 01:12:36.810
So I think there is a
recognition in the document

01:12:36.810 --> 01:12:39.540
about the need to develop skills

01:12:39.540 --> 01:12:41.490
and capabilities for
strategic commissioning

01:12:41.490 --> 01:12:44.280
and the development programme
to go alongside that.

01:12:44.280 --> 01:12:46.200
So I think there are
strategic commissioning skills

01:12:46.200 --> 01:12:48.300
but absolutely we need to build capability

01:12:48.300 --> 01:12:50.660
and capacity and I think
that will be really important

01:12:50.660 --> 01:12:53.313
in the next phase.

01:12:54.680 --> 01:12:55.513
I think the piece Mark

01:12:55.513 --> 01:12:57.540
that you raised is really important

01:12:57.540 --> 01:12:59.880
about the interdependencies
with the development

01:12:59.880 --> 01:13:03.390
of the operating model across
regions and up to the centre.

01:13:03.390 --> 01:13:07.890
We are working very hard on
the model region work as well

01:13:07.890 --> 01:13:10.410
and members of the team sit on both sides

01:13:10.410 --> 01:13:13.020
so that we can help drive alignment

01:13:13.020 --> 01:13:16.170
and as part of the programme to oversee

01:13:16.170 --> 01:13:17.820
the sort of development of this work.

01:13:17.820 --> 01:13:19.260
I think there's a connection point here

01:13:19.260 --> 01:13:21.570
which looks at risks
around that alignment.

01:13:21.570 --> 01:13:23.550
So that's really, really important

01:13:23.550 --> 01:13:25.470
and I think working on that at pace

01:13:25.470 --> 01:13:28.328
is what we are doing.

01:13:28.328 --> 01:13:33.328
Risk is fundamental to what we are doing.

01:13:34.860 --> 01:13:37.352
Risk management is so
the document highlights

01:13:37.352 --> 01:13:40.035
on what we're sort of expecting

01:13:40.035 --> 01:13:40.920
in terms of safe transition

01:13:40.920 --> 01:13:43.300
and that we've got a programme of work

01:13:44.250 --> 01:13:47.481
sort of coordination central role Andrew

01:13:47.481 --> 01:13:48.954
that is looking at the risks

01:13:48.954 --> 01:13:50.340
and the templates that have been sent out

01:13:50.340 --> 01:13:52.140
ask to highlight the
risks that we're seeing.

01:13:52.140 --> 01:13:54.510
So absolutely, we will be looking

01:13:54.510 --> 01:13:58.810
at what the plans show us and
to do the support of check

01:13:59.732 --> 01:14:00.870
and challenge, do the bit
around sharing best practise

01:14:00.870 --> 01:14:04.170
but also to escalate the
risk that we need to see.

01:14:04.170 --> 01:14:07.710
There will be a comprehensive
risk register that supports up

01:14:07.710 --> 01:14:10.016
and leads onto our
strategic risk register.

01:14:10.016 --> 01:14:12.780
I think that's really, really important

01:14:13.801 --> 01:14:18.801
in terms of the working
with local government,

01:14:19.617 --> 01:14:22.170
that's been really great for me,

01:14:22.170 --> 01:14:26.280
I think there's real sense of desire

01:14:26.280 --> 01:14:28.590
from local government
to work closely with us,

01:14:28.590 --> 01:14:31.860
particularly on the areas
of safeguarding, SEND

01:14:31.860 --> 01:14:36.860
and CHC. I did a webinar
attended by 292 I think,

01:14:38.220 --> 01:14:41.340
colleagues earlier this
week about really interested

01:14:41.340 --> 01:14:44.280
in this work and wanting
to make sure they play in.

01:14:44.280 --> 01:14:47.290
So I think it will be a real opportunity

01:14:48.290 --> 01:14:49.947
over the coming weeks and months

01:14:49.947 --> 01:14:51.490
to think about how do we work

01:14:51.490 --> 01:14:52.890
through the best solution for those areas.

01:14:52.890 --> 01:14:54.360
And picking up on your point Mary,

01:14:54.360 --> 01:14:56.670
I'll need to make sure how we link that in

01:14:56.670 --> 01:15:00.176
because I think there's
this a real sense of desire

01:15:00.176 --> 01:15:04.957
to get this right, this
is really important

01:15:04.957 --> 01:15:07.770
and as Duncan says around
the risks, you know,

01:15:07.770 --> 01:15:09.600
the statutory responsibilities

01:15:09.600 --> 01:15:13.020
for these areas are quite complex.

01:15:13.020 --> 01:15:14.830
We need to manage really carefully

01:15:16.087 --> 01:15:18.993
and some things will
need legislative change.

01:15:19.966 --> 01:15:23.490
So we need to step through
this with the right experience

01:15:23.490 --> 01:15:25.594
around the table to get it right

01:15:25.594 --> 01:15:28.590
and to make sure that we as
NHS England have the line

01:15:28.590 --> 01:15:32.913
of sight of those
responsibilities as they transfer.

01:15:34.411 --> 01:15:35.730
Have I answered all the questions Penny?

01:15:35.730 --> 01:15:38.040
- You haven't explicitly
spoken about the money.

01:15:38.040 --> 01:15:40.023
I might hand that to Jim.

01:15:41.197 --> 01:15:43.318
So I think Jim if you
could pick up on, you know,

01:15:43.318 --> 01:15:44.730
how do we give ourselves, well A,

01:15:44.730 --> 01:15:47.610
and Miranda has answered some of this

01:15:47.610 --> 01:15:50.984
as people send back their plans tomorrow,

01:15:50.984 --> 01:15:51.840
we'll start to see how this is looking.

01:15:51.840 --> 01:15:53.637
I think there is a fundamental question

01:15:53.637 --> 01:15:57.263
and a concern about how we ensure

01:15:57.263 --> 01:16:00.229
that ICBs are retaining
the right sort of skills

01:16:00.229 --> 01:16:02.190
and capabilities and the right functions

01:16:02.190 --> 01:16:05.325
in line with the way
in which the model ICB

01:16:05.325 --> 01:16:07.583
has been described,

01:16:07.583 --> 01:16:11.310
and also at the same time
exactly to Duncan's point,

01:16:11.310 --> 01:16:13.641
how are we assuring
ourselves that the functions

01:16:13.641 --> 01:16:15.591
that are being transferred

01:16:15.591 --> 01:16:17.280
are being effectively transferred,

01:16:17.280 --> 01:16:19.669
recognising that some of these things

01:16:19.669 --> 01:16:21.677
need quite substantive relook like CHC

01:16:21.677 --> 01:16:23.869
being a good example, so Jim.

01:16:23.869 --> 01:16:25.890
- Elizabeth will chip
in on the money as well.

01:16:25.890 --> 01:16:29.280
So I mean there's there's
no way of dressing this up.

01:16:29.280 --> 01:16:32.790
It's a really big complicated risky change

01:16:32.790 --> 01:16:36.510
driven in large part by the
circumstances that we're in.

01:16:36.510 --> 01:16:39.660
And on your point, Andrew, if
you go back a couple of years,

01:16:39.660 --> 01:16:41.240
we weren't spending as much

01:16:41.240 --> 01:16:45.360
on ICBs a few years
ago than as we are now.

01:16:45.360 --> 01:16:47.910
There's been growth, the
whole of service has grown

01:16:48.838 --> 01:16:50.070
in corporate functions

01:16:50.070 --> 01:16:54.480
and corporate costs certainly
since the COVID period.

01:16:54.480 --> 01:16:57.060
And we've ended up with
a very, very complicated

01:16:57.060 --> 01:16:59.250
operating model, in my trust life,

01:16:59.250 --> 01:17:02.550
you would very often be
in discussions with two,

01:17:02.550 --> 01:17:07.550
sometimes four layers of the
NHS talking about an issue

01:17:07.950 --> 01:17:09.720
that in the old days would've been solved

01:17:09.720 --> 01:17:12.360
by one layer, two layers max.

01:17:12.360 --> 01:17:16.167
And we all understand that,
we've all lived with it.

01:17:16.167 --> 01:17:18.690
It was all same for
good reason at the time,

01:17:18.690 --> 01:17:20.790
but the circumstances have changed.

01:17:20.790 --> 01:17:24.052
So we've had to lean in with that,

01:17:24.052 --> 01:17:26.750
but we are having to do an
awful lot quite quickly.

01:17:26.750 --> 01:17:28.800
I think on that the level of engagement

01:17:28.800 --> 01:17:30.780
with ICB colleagues has been fantastic

01:17:30.780 --> 01:17:33.210
and their energy and response

01:17:33.210 --> 01:17:35.357
and willingness to participate

01:17:35.357 --> 01:17:37.808
and shape has been really good.

01:17:37.808 --> 01:17:39.266
Penny, I know you met
with colleagues yesterday

01:17:39.266 --> 01:17:41.040
and there's still a
degree of anxiety there

01:17:41.040 --> 01:17:45.540
because you know, in part they
want to see the whole picture

01:17:45.540 --> 01:17:46.983
how everything fits.

01:17:47.971 --> 01:17:51.513
We are making informed guesses
as to how everything fits.

01:17:52.381 --> 01:17:54.532
So I think we can land that properly

01:17:54.532 --> 01:17:57.670
'cause a lot of us are involved
in the other discussions,

01:17:57.670 --> 01:17:59.710
but we need to just keep
checking at every spot

01:17:59.710 --> 01:18:01.062
that we haven't missed something.

01:18:01.062 --> 01:18:02.490
We're not going to do damage,
we're not going to remove

01:18:02.490 --> 01:18:05.170
or disable the key part of the system

01:18:05.170 --> 01:18:06.826
that then is going to cause us a problem.

01:18:06.826 --> 01:18:09.364
And that's a very interactive thing.

01:18:09.364 --> 01:18:12.194
Throughout the whole, and
this works for our integration

01:18:12.194 --> 01:18:13.500
as well at a national level,

01:18:13.500 --> 01:18:18.500
the 50% thing was in the main, a signal

01:18:18.870 --> 01:18:21.967
that this is a significant
change that we need.

01:18:21.967 --> 01:18:25.200
It's not tinkering at the
edges, in the end of it's 45

01:18:25.200 --> 01:18:27.243
or 52 or you know, whatever.

01:18:28.110 --> 01:18:30.390
It's going to be the best we'll land on.

01:18:30.390 --> 01:18:33.750
We're all going to be
pragmatic about that.

01:18:33.750 --> 01:18:35.970
But it can't be something
where we tinker at the edges

01:18:35.970 --> 01:18:38.468
and come back and it's 15%

01:18:38.468 --> 01:18:39.360
and we've just left a lot of the overlap

01:18:39.360 --> 01:18:40.440
and a lot of the confusion

01:18:40.440 --> 01:18:43.800
and as we were exchanging
on the way down Penny,

01:18:43.800 --> 01:18:46.950
our decision making and approval processes

01:18:46.950 --> 01:18:50.436
are a nightmare to navigate now,

01:18:50.436 --> 01:18:53.010
there's so many people now
involved in decision making

01:18:53.010 --> 01:18:55.500
so that in its own right
means we've got to do this

01:18:55.500 --> 01:18:57.150
but we've got to do it as well as we can.

01:18:57.150 --> 01:18:59.580
And several people have
helpfully mentioned,

01:18:59.580 --> 01:19:01.740
there are a lot of
people involved in this.

01:19:01.740 --> 01:19:05.760
It can feel very much like a
technical transaction process.

01:19:05.760 --> 01:19:08.550
There's a lot of very anxious
people, understandably

01:19:08.550 --> 01:19:11.460
so in a very tight employment market,

01:19:11.460 --> 01:19:13.410
very high cost of living.

01:19:13.410 --> 01:19:16.498
And the sooner I won't come
out the other end of this

01:19:16.498 --> 01:19:18.210
and get people settled as possible

01:19:18.210 --> 01:19:20.790
and help people move into new lives

01:19:20.790 --> 01:19:22.890
and new employment as best we can as well.

01:19:24.510 --> 01:19:27.150
- Just just to add, I guess
going back to the purpose,

01:19:27.150 --> 01:19:30.240
as well, so since just
before the pandemic,

01:19:30.240 --> 01:19:32.700
the corporate costs in providers alone

01:19:32.700 --> 01:19:35.550
grew by 1.8 billion pounds

01:19:35.550 --> 01:19:38.763
that could have been spent
on direct healthcare.

01:19:38.763 --> 01:19:41.400
And at the start when ICBs
were first established,

01:19:41.400 --> 01:19:42.960
their running cost per head of population

01:19:42.960 --> 01:19:45.483
was between 20 and 25 pounds a head.

01:19:46.410 --> 01:19:50.712
As of today, we've got some
that are over 70 pounds a head.

01:19:50.712 --> 01:19:52.287
So the variation in there,

01:19:52.287 --> 01:19:54.900
so we've not said everybody's
going to cut by 50%.

01:19:54.900 --> 01:19:57.660
We've looked at, if
you add it all together

01:19:57.660 --> 01:20:00.786
and then you look at a macro level,

01:20:00.786 --> 01:20:03.150
reducing an aggregate
down to a 50% target,

01:20:03.150 --> 01:20:04.920
what does that look like?

01:20:04.920 --> 01:20:06.810
And effectively some costs were charged

01:20:06.810 --> 01:20:07.980
to something we would describe

01:20:07.980 --> 01:20:11.703
as programme costs which deliver
direct patient healthcare.

01:20:13.236 --> 01:20:16.380
But I would also say,

01:20:16.380 --> 01:20:18.990
there are some fantastic ICBs out there

01:20:18.990 --> 01:20:20.490
doing some fantastic work.

01:20:20.490 --> 01:20:23.790
So this is quite a nuanced piece of work

01:20:23.790 --> 01:20:25.770
that I know Penny's guiding us through

01:20:25.770 --> 01:20:27.622
with the model ICB,

01:20:27.622 --> 01:20:28.620
which is why it's taken
a little bit more time

01:20:28.620 --> 01:20:30.150
because we'd all starting

01:20:30.150 --> 01:20:32.370
from a very different starting place.

01:20:32.370 --> 01:20:34.520
It comes back to that
word variation again.

01:20:35.730 --> 01:20:38.590
- But I just wanted to
make the point again really

01:20:38.590 --> 01:20:40.213
that this is more than, as it were,

01:20:40.213 --> 01:20:41.663
a reduction in scale.

01:20:41.663 --> 01:20:44.107
It's actually a total
transformation in what's done

01:20:44.107 --> 01:20:45.720
and that's where the risk lies actually.

01:20:45.720 --> 01:20:47.910
So you end up with half the people

01:20:47.910 --> 01:20:49.500
but not necessarily the right skills.

01:20:49.500 --> 01:20:52.200
- Yeah, so we are very conscious of that.

01:20:52.200 --> 01:20:53.190
I'll just say one thing.

01:20:53.190 --> 01:20:58.190
So yeah, as we are all saying,

01:20:58.836 --> 01:21:00.360
this is a big piece of work

01:21:00.360 --> 01:21:05.087
and we'll come on at the
same time we've got a number

01:21:05.087 --> 01:21:06.249
of very, very big pieces of work.

01:21:06.249 --> 01:21:09.840
So it is not straightforward.

01:21:09.840 --> 01:21:13.001
What has been, I've found though very,

01:21:13.001 --> 01:21:14.820
both a bit of interesting

01:21:14.820 --> 01:21:18.870
but also very positive

01:21:18.870 --> 01:21:22.200
is a substantive proportion

01:21:22.200 --> 01:21:24.420
of ICB chief execs and chairs,

01:21:24.420 --> 01:21:28.052
IE over half, well over half have said

01:21:28.052 --> 01:21:33.052
we're very excited by this
description of a future model.

01:21:33.477 --> 01:21:35.580
This is what we thought
we came into an ICB to do.

01:21:35.580 --> 01:21:38.140
We've been frustrated that
we've been essentially

01:21:39.030 --> 01:21:41.760
working on things that we
didn't think we came in to do

01:21:41.760 --> 01:21:44.190
and this is exactly what we
thought we came into this role

01:21:44.190 --> 01:21:47.010
to do and we are energised
and excited about.

01:21:47.010 --> 01:21:49.128
So I think that has been

01:21:49.128 --> 01:21:52.890
really quite pleasantly surprised by that.

01:21:52.890 --> 01:21:57.450
So I think we have got a sort
of mood music for change,

01:21:57.450 --> 01:21:59.250
but as per the conversation,

01:21:59.250 --> 01:22:02.100
we need to absolutely
make this as risk-free

01:22:02.100 --> 01:22:04.400
and as smooth a transition
as we possibly can.

01:22:05.440 --> 01:22:08.700
Okay, so that then takes us

01:22:08.700 --> 01:22:12.840
neatly onto the integration
work at the centre,

01:22:12.840 --> 01:22:15.840
which as we are continually
reminded by Secretary

01:22:15.840 --> 01:22:18.390
of State, also needs to
include transformation.

01:22:18.390 --> 01:22:21.298
So it's not just bringing together teams

01:22:21.298 --> 01:22:22.131
to work in the same way.

01:22:22.131 --> 01:22:23.520
It's actually to very much change the way

01:22:23.520 --> 01:22:24.933
in which the teams work.

01:22:26.469 --> 01:22:27.302
So Jim.

01:22:27.302 --> 01:22:28.540
- Yeah, so just a few headlines,

01:22:29.400 --> 01:22:30.930
like the earlier item,

01:22:30.930 --> 01:22:33.480
it's a very complicated,
difficult process.

01:22:33.480 --> 01:22:36.730
We've made some significant progress

01:22:37.851 --> 01:22:39.502
in the last couple of weeks

01:22:39.502 --> 01:22:44.356
on just about agreeing the
high level exec structure

01:22:44.356 --> 01:22:46.410
but to underpin that as well,

01:22:46.410 --> 01:22:50.793
the operating model, the style
of working, what's shared,

01:22:50.793 --> 01:22:51.626
what's not shared, you know,

01:22:51.626 --> 01:22:55.767
all those sorts of things
with the aim of being able

01:22:55.767 --> 01:22:58.494
to share this more fully with
colleagues in NHS England

01:22:58.494 --> 01:23:01.290
and DHSE in mid-June together.

01:23:01.290 --> 01:23:03.840
And then as part of
that also the next steps

01:23:03.840 --> 01:23:06.690
to which services will come together

01:23:06.690 --> 01:23:09.540
and integrate over the first wave,

01:23:09.540 --> 01:23:11.620
which will be sort of broadly between now

01:23:12.654 --> 01:23:14.661
and the end of September.

01:23:14.661 --> 01:23:16.260
Also quite a lot of discussion about this.

01:23:16.260 --> 01:23:18.780
Not everything will be shared obviously,

01:23:18.780 --> 01:23:20.702
there'll be some functions

01:23:20.702 --> 01:23:22.950
that are almost entirely NHS facing.

01:23:22.950 --> 01:23:26.020
There's almost entirely Whitehall facing

01:23:26.020 --> 01:23:26.940
and then, you know, there's clearly

01:23:27.831 --> 01:23:29.165
an overlap in the middle.

01:23:29.165 --> 01:23:31.019
So again, I think made quite a lot

01:23:31.019 --> 01:23:33.091
of progress there in recent weeks.

01:23:33.091 --> 01:23:33.924
These things always take longer

01:23:33.924 --> 01:23:35.508
than you want them to as well.

01:23:35.508 --> 01:23:38.031
So I would wish I had, you
know, that would've been

01:23:38.031 --> 01:23:40.325
where we are now with a few weeks ago.

01:23:40.325 --> 01:23:42.240
But it's been very, very complex change

01:23:42.240 --> 01:23:44.240
in Perm Sec and various

01:23:44.240 --> 01:23:46.498
other things going on in the department.

01:23:46.498 --> 01:23:49.320
And also to facilitate this, we've managed

01:23:49.320 --> 01:23:52.707
after several weeks of discussion
with government colleagues

01:23:52.707 --> 01:23:55.020
to agree a voluntary redundancy scheme,

01:23:55.020 --> 01:23:58.740
which were just about open
across the organisation

01:23:58.740 --> 01:24:02.290
to help people make choices for themselves

01:24:03.210 --> 01:24:06.028
and enable a change process.

01:24:06.028 --> 01:24:07.470
So that's not completely nailed down

01:24:07.470 --> 01:24:10.624
in terms of how it's funded
and resourced, et cetera.

01:24:10.624 --> 01:24:12.788
But I think an important component

01:24:12.788 --> 01:24:14.850
to help people move forward.

01:24:14.850 --> 01:24:18.313
And just on the points
earlier raised about risk,

01:24:18.313 --> 01:24:19.170
we're very, very, very focused

01:24:19.170 --> 01:24:21.990
on not dropping a ball in this process.

01:24:21.990 --> 01:24:24.690
Not losing focus on anything important,

01:24:24.690 --> 01:24:27.180
making sure we've got our
attention in the right places

01:24:27.180 --> 01:24:29.130
and we actually don't disable something

01:24:29.130 --> 01:24:33.110
that's actually very, very
important to our operating.

01:24:33.110 --> 01:24:35.010
But at the same time,
taking the opportunity

01:24:35.010 --> 01:24:39.787
to change the operating model,
make it cleaner, quicker,

01:24:39.787 --> 01:24:43.620
more agile, less complex
in terms of decision making

01:24:43.620 --> 01:24:46.443
and more supportive to
colleagues out in the front line.

01:24:47.875 --> 01:24:49.223
- Great, and Rob, do you want

01:24:49.223 --> 01:24:51.030
to comment on the comms around this?

01:24:51.030 --> 01:24:53.994
- Yes, and I think slightly,

01:24:53.994 --> 01:24:55.920
I think it's been slightly
slower than I'd have hoped

01:24:55.920 --> 01:24:58.606
in the last few weeks
getting all that together.

01:24:58.606 --> 01:24:59.640
But that's part of the broader complexity

01:24:59.640 --> 01:25:01.550
that Jim's described.

01:25:01.550 --> 01:25:05.160
We've actually got
really good relationships

01:25:05.160 --> 01:25:07.539
with the department comms team.

01:25:07.539 --> 01:25:08.610
So now jointly working

01:25:08.610 --> 01:25:12.540
with the leadership on this
programme to create something

01:25:12.540 --> 01:25:15.300
that's really open, really honest

01:25:15.300 --> 01:25:16.772
where we've got answers,

01:25:16.772 --> 01:25:20.569
let's get on the front foot with that,

01:25:20.569 --> 01:25:22.200
where we haven't, let's
be honest about it.

01:25:22.200 --> 01:25:25.830
Recognising in all of
that, both a genuine sense

01:25:25.830 --> 01:25:28.020
of opportunity for some folks

01:25:28.020 --> 01:25:31.620
who are really quite excited
about the change in direction,

01:25:31.620 --> 01:25:34.290
but also recognising that
for some other people,

01:25:34.290 --> 01:25:36.720
this is the second or third time

01:25:36.720 --> 01:25:39.677
they've been around this
kind of reorganising

01:25:39.677 --> 01:25:40.510
really short period of time.

01:25:40.510 --> 01:25:42.360
So we have to understand the pain

01:25:42.360 --> 01:25:43.825
that this is all causing

01:25:43.825 --> 01:25:46.170
and be really careful in how we're talking

01:25:46.170 --> 01:25:48.630
about what we're, talking about

01:25:48.630 --> 01:25:51.600
to make sure that they're, you know,

01:25:51.600 --> 01:25:54.183
they're okay in this process as well.

01:25:56.370 --> 01:25:57.203
- Mark.

01:25:58.548 --> 01:26:01.860
- To your comment about transformation,

01:26:01.860 --> 01:26:04.892
clearly quite a lot of the tale

01:26:04.892 --> 01:26:06.602
of activity here relates
to statutory duties

01:26:06.602 --> 01:26:09.363
of which a year ago when I joined,

01:26:10.489 --> 01:26:14.620
I was surprised by the sheer number scope

01:26:15.750 --> 01:26:19.228
and in fact have, well the
scope in aggregate of them,

01:26:19.228 --> 01:26:22.006
but the narrow scope
of quite a lot of them,

01:26:22.006 --> 01:26:23.972
is that on the table?

01:26:25.170 --> 01:26:26.926
- Oh yes, Jim.

01:26:30.418 --> 01:26:31.900
- Everything's on the table,

01:26:31.900 --> 01:26:33.525
Mark, absolutely,
everything's on the table

01:26:33.525 --> 01:26:35.190
and as part of this, there's
some really quite tricky

01:26:35.190 --> 01:26:37.893
discussions about, it might sit here now,

01:26:38.792 --> 01:26:40.447
there might have been
a good reason for that,

01:26:40.447 --> 01:26:42.150
but it might actually
more appropriately sit

01:26:42.150 --> 01:26:43.680
somewhere else in the landscape.

01:26:43.680 --> 01:26:46.080
I think we'd want to look as broad

01:26:46.080 --> 01:26:49.200
as we can across the whole
of the ALB architecture,

01:26:49.200 --> 01:26:50.673
right into the service,

01:26:51.512 --> 01:26:54.988
make sure everything is in
the right place effectively

01:26:54.988 --> 01:26:56.775
as part of all this.

01:26:56.775 --> 01:27:00.011
And then especially in the areas
you are interested in most,

01:27:00.011 --> 01:27:01.474
make sure we've got the right resilience,

01:27:01.474 --> 01:27:03.030
we've actually got the security we need

01:27:03.030 --> 01:27:06.630
to be able to perform
these functions properly.

01:27:06.630 --> 01:27:07.677
Key to all of that,

01:27:07.677 --> 01:27:11.070
and Penny and other colleagues did a lot

01:27:11.070 --> 01:27:12.770
of work on this at the
beginning of this process,

01:27:12.770 --> 01:27:14.491
they're trying to distil what
we're here for in the centre

01:27:14.491 --> 01:27:16.740
and these things change over time,

01:27:16.740 --> 01:27:18.780
but just trying to really
get back the basics,

01:27:18.780 --> 01:27:21.360
what can we only do here and therefore

01:27:21.360 --> 01:27:24.930
after that there's choices
about where everything else sits

01:27:24.930 --> 01:27:26.551
and we've just got to make that

01:27:26.551 --> 01:27:28.443
as an informed process as we can do.

01:27:29.722 --> 01:27:32.442
- And as well as that,
moving things around

01:27:32.442 --> 01:27:37.110
clearly as you get quality improvement,

01:27:37.110 --> 01:27:38.767
risk culture improvement,

01:27:38.767 --> 01:27:41.370
first line accountability improvement,

01:27:41.370 --> 01:27:43.792
some of the statute duties are

01:27:43.792 --> 01:27:45.420
actually just a replication
of what would be

01:27:45.420 --> 01:27:46.860
in a normal control environment.

01:27:46.860 --> 01:27:48.150
- Absolutely.

01:27:48.150 --> 01:27:51.510
- And therefore as long as
that requires political will

01:27:51.510 --> 01:27:53.640
'cause these all things were decided on

01:27:53.640 --> 01:27:55.540
and voted on at some point by someone.

01:27:57.070 --> 01:27:59.932
- Not quite sure on the voted on

01:27:59.932 --> 01:28:02.760
obviously decided, it got in there.

01:28:02.760 --> 01:28:03.593
- Somehow arrived.

01:28:03.593 --> 01:28:04.426
- Yes, exactly.

01:28:05.293 --> 01:28:07.560
So just to add a bit more

01:28:07.560 --> 01:28:10.016
in terms of the governance
structure around this.

01:28:10.016 --> 01:28:12.090
So there is a transformation programme,

01:28:12.090 --> 01:28:15.660
transformation board,
which I am co-chairing

01:28:15.660 --> 01:28:17.820
with Alan Milburn who's the lead NED

01:28:17.820 --> 01:28:19.755
in the department of health.

01:28:19.755 --> 01:28:24.630
We've then got Jim and now
Sam Jones sitting on that.

01:28:24.630 --> 01:28:27.150
And Sam has got much more involved

01:28:27.150 --> 01:28:30.150
in this over the last few
weeks, which has really helped.

01:28:30.150 --> 01:28:31.710
We've been able to make some decisions

01:28:31.710 --> 01:28:35.474
which was a bit hard in
a interregnum period.

01:28:35.474 --> 01:28:40.440
We brought Richard Barker in
to help to lead this day to day

01:28:40.440 --> 01:28:42.840
and he is supported by a team of people

01:28:42.840 --> 01:28:46.940
from both DHSC and NHSE
and one of the things

01:28:46.940 --> 01:28:50.250
that they have done very
dutifully is both a full mapping

01:28:50.250 --> 01:28:52.560
of all of the people and all of the teams,

01:28:52.560 --> 01:28:55.570
but also a full mapping of all
of those statutory functions.

01:28:55.570 --> 01:28:57.720
Now that will obviously take some time

01:28:57.720 --> 01:29:00.570
for us to work through
it all, but I think,

01:29:00.570 --> 01:29:04.620
and I hope we have now resolved both,

01:29:04.620 --> 01:29:07.672
we've now agreed on A, what's the purpose

01:29:07.672 --> 01:29:11.340
and B, what are the functions and C,

01:29:11.340 --> 01:29:14.943
therefore what does the top
team as it were look like?

01:29:16.530 --> 01:29:17.670
It has taken us a bit longer

01:29:17.670 --> 01:29:19.590
than we'd hoped, but
we have now done that.

01:29:19.590 --> 01:29:23.806
And when will that be sort
of announced, published?

01:29:23.806 --> 01:29:25.071
- Mid-June we're aiming for

01:29:25.071 --> 01:29:27.363
so we haven't nailed the day down exactly.

01:29:28.216 --> 01:29:30.032
So a bit of work to do to do that.

01:29:30.032 --> 01:29:31.736
We wanted to an joint all staff briefing

01:29:31.736 --> 01:29:35.487
and all us, we be able
to explain all of that.

01:29:35.487 --> 01:29:36.720
Then we're just very aware, Penny,

01:29:36.720 --> 01:29:39.300
from the all staff
briefings we've done so far,

01:29:39.300 --> 01:29:43.055
people want to know more
than we can say now.

01:29:43.055 --> 01:29:44.404
So we've just got to get the balance right

01:29:44.404 --> 01:29:45.780
of keeping people informed

01:29:45.780 --> 01:29:47.610
but also not raising expectation.

01:29:47.610 --> 01:29:50.906
'Cause if you're an individual
involved in this 15,000

01:29:50.906 --> 01:29:52.770
all time equivalence here, you
want to know what that means

01:29:52.770 --> 01:29:56.762
for you and when you want at that level

01:29:56.762 --> 01:29:57.690
of precision just yet.

01:29:57.690 --> 01:29:59.310
So about getting that balance right.

01:29:59.310 --> 01:30:01.976
But mid-June is the point
we're aiming for that.

01:30:01.976 --> 01:30:04.107
- Penny, does the work include

01:30:04.107 --> 01:30:06.240
the kind of powers of delegation?

01:30:06.240 --> 01:30:10.230
Because if you come from
a trust to NHS England,

01:30:10.230 --> 01:30:13.112
you notice how different it is.

01:30:13.112 --> 01:30:14.955
I mean you obviously see this day in,

01:30:14.955 --> 01:30:18.930
day out, in the trust, the chief
exec can make any decision,

01:30:18.930 --> 01:30:21.780
you know, if it's backed by the board

01:30:21.780 --> 01:30:24.300
and you've got a hell
of a lot of authority.

01:30:24.300 --> 01:30:27.210
At NHS England, it doesn't work like that.

01:30:27.210 --> 01:30:31.980
And it is always frustrated me
to just, you know, understand

01:30:31.980 --> 01:30:33.480
where the power base is

01:30:33.480 --> 01:30:37.710
and I think that's the
important conversation

01:30:37.710 --> 01:30:42.177
to be had here because if
the new DH is to be agile,

01:30:42.177 --> 01:30:46.890
it needs to have people who are empowered.

01:30:46.890 --> 01:30:50.640
And we haven't quite
got that at the moment.

01:30:50.640 --> 01:30:53.790
So going forward, I hope
that is a key feature

01:30:53.790 --> 01:30:57.417
because if it isn't, we're
just replicating what we've got

01:30:57.417 --> 01:30:59.100
and that isn't good enough really.

01:30:59.100 --> 01:31:01.500
- It's been a key part

01:31:01.500 --> 01:31:03.217
of our interactions over the last 48 hours

01:31:03.217 --> 01:31:05.670
about just at every point.

01:31:05.670 --> 01:31:07.980
And as well, not just waiting
for the change process

01:31:07.980 --> 01:31:10.080
to do this, at every point

01:31:10.080 --> 01:31:13.610
asking ourselves why do five committees

01:31:13.610 --> 01:31:15.210
or groups have to sign this off?

01:31:15.210 --> 01:31:17.589
You know, we need to be
able to trust people,

01:31:17.589 --> 01:31:19.318
we need to have people in place

01:31:19.318 --> 01:31:22.047
we can trust, the systems need
to be around them to do that.

01:31:22.047 --> 01:31:23.430
And there's an awful lot built

01:31:23.430 --> 01:31:25.440
into how we work here.

01:31:25.440 --> 01:31:28.863
Some of it's government controls as well.

01:31:28.863 --> 01:31:31.218
So we do have to understand we work

01:31:31.218 --> 01:31:32.279
in a broader government context,

01:31:32.279 --> 01:31:33.510
but an awful lot of it's self created

01:31:33.510 --> 01:31:34.710
and it's very constrained.

01:31:34.710 --> 01:31:37.142
I mean, Mark and other
colleagues who've come in

01:31:37.142 --> 01:31:38.520
from outside, I would say
you've lived here before,

01:31:38.520 --> 01:31:40.413
Mark, but drives you mad.

01:31:41.460 --> 01:31:42.957
- I can understand that.

01:31:42.957 --> 01:31:44.603
- Can I just say, I mean,

01:31:44.603 --> 01:31:48.210
I think that empowerment
also makes clear to people

01:31:48.210 --> 01:31:50.190
what their accountability is

01:31:50.190 --> 01:31:52.590
and that's what people
really want to know.

01:31:52.590 --> 01:31:54.330
Am I accountable for this?

01:31:54.330 --> 01:31:56.640
In which case, you know, I'm
going to stay up all night

01:31:56.640 --> 01:31:59.250
and sort it out or have I got five

01:31:59.250 --> 01:32:01.597
other people I have to work with?

01:32:01.597 --> 01:32:02.651
- Absolutely.

01:32:02.651 --> 01:32:03.484
We've got to build that
right through the service.

01:32:03.484 --> 01:32:05.352
So one of the things
we've been talking about,

01:32:05.352 --> 01:32:07.080
we've been talking about
maternity a lot recently

01:32:07.080 --> 01:32:09.150
and our concerns about maternity.

01:32:09.150 --> 01:32:12.060
That's a very complex environment now

01:32:12.060 --> 01:32:16.710
with the different mechanisms
to oversee in colleagues.

01:32:16.710 --> 01:32:19.920
You know, I felt this
when I was in the trust,

01:32:19.920 --> 01:32:23.070
feel less accountable and
less able to influence

01:32:23.070 --> 01:32:24.870
'cause there's all these
external influences.

01:32:24.870 --> 01:32:26.910
In all of our work, we've got
to make sure accountability

01:32:26.910 --> 01:32:28.380
sits in the right place.

01:32:28.380 --> 01:32:30.930
The rules are clear, it's
fair, it's proportionate.

01:32:30.930 --> 01:32:33.540
There's a response when
something doesn't work.

01:32:33.540 --> 01:32:36.780
- Yeah, so on a positive.

01:32:38.460 --> 01:32:43.460
I am optimistic that when we can see

01:32:44.730 --> 01:32:46.713
the new structure of the centre,

01:32:48.510 --> 01:32:50.310
back to purpose and the functions

01:32:50.310 --> 01:32:54.930
and the structure, when we have
published the 10 year plan,

01:32:54.930 --> 01:32:57.900
which includes essentially
a target operating model

01:32:57.900 --> 01:33:02.900
for regions, ICBs and providers,

01:33:03.150 --> 01:33:05.970
including primary care providers.

01:33:05.970 --> 01:33:08.520
And we have the 10 year plan
with the future strategy.

01:33:08.520 --> 01:33:10.170
Now of course all that's
the wrong way round.

01:33:10.170 --> 01:33:13.660
We need to know the strategy
in order to inform all of that.

01:33:13.660 --> 01:33:14.730
But that will all be together

01:33:14.730 --> 01:33:18.390
by hopefully early July at the latest.

01:33:18.390 --> 01:33:20.030
We should have all of that together.

01:33:20.030 --> 01:33:24.250
And I think it will, I'm
optimistic that it will

01:33:25.650 --> 01:33:28.710
essentially paint a far clearer landscape

01:33:28.710 --> 01:33:30.540
of what happens where and who does what

01:33:30.540 --> 01:33:32.591
and how does it all fit together.

01:33:32.591 --> 01:33:37.591
So as long as we can get
through the next six weeks,

01:33:38.310 --> 01:33:39.810
that should be a better place.

01:33:41.287 --> 01:33:43.650
Okay, good, so final item

01:33:43.650 --> 01:33:48.650
is we need to approve
a statement, Elizabeth.

01:33:49.720 --> 01:33:52.085
- Yeah, very happy to pick
this up, so thank you.

01:33:52.085 --> 01:33:52.980
I'm really pleased to
present this actually

01:33:52.980 --> 01:33:55.535
on behalf of NHS England.

01:33:55.535 --> 01:33:59.370
Clearly a set of actions
to address modern slavery

01:33:59.370 --> 01:34:01.950
and NHS procurement and supply chain.

01:34:01.950 --> 01:34:04.920
There's a great team of people,
really passionate people

01:34:04.920 --> 01:34:06.900
that are working on this
agenda on our behalf

01:34:06.900 --> 01:34:09.690
and working out into the service
with our procurement leads.

01:34:09.690 --> 01:34:12.360
So if you read the paper

01:34:12.360 --> 01:34:16.830
and some of the background
information, it's moving.

01:34:16.830 --> 01:34:19.230
It's something that we
know we need to address

01:34:19.230 --> 01:34:21.360
and be on the front foot with.

01:34:21.360 --> 01:34:24.300
So this today is a paper
to update on the progress

01:34:24.300 --> 01:34:27.630
that's been made, approve
the modern slavery statement

01:34:27.630 --> 01:34:30.450
for 2025 ready for publication

01:34:30.450 --> 01:34:33.540
and then also approve
the continued delegation

01:34:33.540 --> 01:34:35.940
for non-material changes to the guidance

01:34:35.940 --> 01:34:39.498
to our chief commercial
officer, Fiona Bride,

01:34:39.498 --> 01:34:41.099
who's been working with the team on it.

01:34:41.099 --> 01:34:43.440
Hopefully you've had a
chance to read the paper.

01:34:43.440 --> 01:34:46.950
So the context of the
background is absolutely clear.

01:34:46.950 --> 01:34:50.400
Modern slavery has really
serious, serious violation

01:34:50.400 --> 01:34:55.200
of human rights and
throughout over the last year,

01:34:55.200 --> 01:34:58.290
we've actually had a 12
week public consultation,

01:34:58.290 --> 01:35:02.730
which concluded in February
really wide participation.

01:35:02.730 --> 01:35:04.650
We thank everybody that's been involved

01:35:04.650 --> 01:35:06.466
and come back to us.

01:35:06.466 --> 01:35:09.090
There wasn't any material changes

01:35:09.090 --> 01:35:11.310
that were made as a
result of the feedback.

01:35:11.310 --> 01:35:12.900
Just a lot of support.

01:35:12.900 --> 01:35:15.630
And also there'll be
a parliamentary debate

01:35:15.630 --> 01:35:20.370
we'd anticipate prior
to the summer recess.

01:35:20.370 --> 01:35:23.700
So delegated authority to
the chief commercial officer

01:35:23.700 --> 01:35:25.470
if that's amenable by the board,

01:35:25.470 --> 01:35:27.120
just to maintain the flexibility

01:35:27.120 --> 01:35:30.993
whilst we are ensuring proper
oversight of this agenda.

01:35:32.714 --> 01:35:34.740
Okay, thank you, any comments?

01:35:34.740 --> 01:35:36.405
Okay.

01:35:36.405 --> 01:35:37.457
- I just go back comments
on the company that,

01:35:37.457 --> 01:35:40.309
I mean I remember sort of so much

01:35:40.309 --> 01:35:41.387
about the background to this

01:35:41.387 --> 01:35:42.930
and how important it is,
but also the incredible role

01:35:42.930 --> 01:35:44.700
that frontline staff play.

01:35:44.700 --> 01:35:46.617
Because actually, you know,

01:35:46.617 --> 01:35:48.965
you can do all the systems
work described here

01:35:48.965 --> 01:35:50.262
around supply chain that,

01:35:50.262 --> 01:35:51.420
but actually a lot of the victims

01:35:51.420 --> 01:35:55.397
of modern slavery are
identified by frontline staff.

01:35:55.397 --> 01:35:57.300
You know, who they come into contact with.

01:35:57.300 --> 01:36:01.050
So I just hope when we present
this we make sure that,

01:36:01.050 --> 01:36:04.530
you know, we don't just
emphasise the importance

01:36:04.530 --> 01:36:07.440
of what you've done around
drilling down a supply chain,

01:36:07.440 --> 01:36:10.500
but also that critical human contact

01:36:10.500 --> 01:36:13.530
that is often the moment where somebody

01:36:13.530 --> 01:36:15.420
who is a victim is identified

01:36:15.420 --> 01:36:17.020
and begins a journey out of that.

01:36:18.391 --> 01:36:20.670
- Thank you, so approval for that.

01:36:20.670 --> 01:36:22.209
Thank you.

01:36:22.209 --> 01:36:25.050
Does anyone have any other
business they'd like to raise?

01:36:25.050 --> 01:36:29.703
Okay, so I'd like to conclude
by, I think reinforcing,

01:36:30.810 --> 01:36:34.530
we have an awful lot of work to be done

01:36:34.530 --> 01:36:37.020
on almost business as usual.

01:36:37.020 --> 01:36:40.560
So well over one and a half million people

01:36:40.560 --> 01:36:41.670
out there in the service

01:36:41.670 --> 01:36:43.713
delivering outstanding care day in,

01:36:44.622 --> 01:36:48.060
day out in often quite
difficult environment.

01:36:48.060 --> 01:36:50.760
A lot of people in ICBs in the regions

01:36:50.760 --> 01:36:53.520
and here who are trying to improve that,

01:36:53.520 --> 01:36:56.850
to make that better day in, day out.

01:36:56.850 --> 01:37:00.120
We have a lot of people from
right across the service

01:37:00.120 --> 01:37:02.370
and members of the public, very involved

01:37:02.370 --> 01:37:04.890
in helping to redesign the service,

01:37:04.890 --> 01:37:07.230
to come up with a compelling

01:37:07.230 --> 01:37:10.195
and implementable 10 year plan.

01:37:10.195 --> 01:37:13.583
We are redesigning integrated care boards

01:37:13.583 --> 01:37:18.120
and making significant changes
as we discussed before,

01:37:18.120 --> 01:37:21.093
and we are changing the
whole model at the centre.

01:37:22.020 --> 01:37:23.670
It's a massive agenda.

01:37:23.670 --> 01:37:25.945
And right across all of that,

01:37:25.945 --> 01:37:29.040
people are working
exceptionally long hours

01:37:29.040 --> 01:37:31.560
putting in way more than
we should really be asking

01:37:31.560 --> 01:37:34.816
and expecting them to,
including, you know, everyone

01:37:34.816 --> 01:37:36.840
around this table and
way beyond this table.

01:37:36.840 --> 01:37:39.060
And I think just to say
thank you to everyone

01:37:39.060 --> 01:37:42.630
for doing that, it's a pretty
tough time that we're in.

01:37:42.630 --> 01:37:45.388
But as I said before,
optimistic that it will start

01:37:45.388 --> 01:37:47.490
to feel clearer and we'll have a sense

01:37:47.490 --> 01:37:51.270
of where we're going by
hopefully the middle of July.

01:37:51.270 --> 01:37:52.917
So thank you all very much.

01:37:52.917 --> 01:37:54.412
Thank you very much to
people who've joined today.

01:37:54.412 --> 01:37:56.353
Thank you very much to people
who are watching on the screen

01:37:56.353 --> 01:37:59.640
and look forward to the
next meeting of this board,

01:37:59.640 --> 01:38:03.148
which is I think on the 17th of July.

01:38:03.148 --> 01:38:04.653
So thank you very much.

